// Evidence types help content for assessment questions
// Structured as: { "QUESTION-CODE": "evidence help text" }

export const helpContent = {
  "FA-1": "To demonstrate compliance with **FA-1** **(\"Identify and mitigate bias\nin AI systems\")**, look for evidence showing both **proactive** and\n**reactive** bias-mitigation measures across the AI lifecycle.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Governance:** A Bias Mitigation Policy or Fairness Framework outlining responsibilities, oversight committees, and escalation processes for bias incidents.\n\n- **Data & Model Practices:** Documentation proving datasets are diverse and representative, preprocessing steps to rebalance or anonymize data, and fairness testing or audits of models using tools such as *Fairlearn*, *AI Fairness 360*, *SHAP*, or *LIME*.\n\n- **Training & Awareness:** Records of staff training or workshops on bias awareness and ethical AI practices.\n\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Ongoing fairness monitoring, KPIs or metrics (e.g., demographic parity, equal opportunity), and regular internal or external reporting on bias management.\n\n- **Independent Validation:** Results of third-party bias audits or fairness certifications.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires documented processes, technical\nevidence of fairness testing, clear accountability, and continuous\nmonitoring. Gaps typically arise when bias detection is treated as a\none-off activity rather than an ongoing governance commitment.",
  "FA-2": "To comply with **FA-2** **(\"Ensure training data represents all relevant\ndemographics\")**, look for evidence that training datasets are\n**diverse, representative, and regularly reviewed** for inclusivity.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Governance:** A documented *Data Representation Policy* and *Diversity Standards* outlining minimum demographic inclusion requirements, with oversight by a fairness or data governance committee.\n\n- **Data Collection & Validation:** Proof that data is sourced from multiple regions, demographics, or contexts; validation of historical datasets for completeness and bias; and stakeholder or expert review to confirm representativeness.\n\n- **Technical Controls:** Evidence of *data balancing*, *synthetic data generation*, and use of bias-detection tools (e.g., *Fairlearn*, *AI Fairness 360*). Metrics such as demographic parity or representation indices should be used to measure fairness.\n\n- **Documentation & Audits:** Dataset composition reports showing demographic breakdowns, versioning with metadata, and regular internal or third-party audits assessing demographic inclusivity.\n\n- **Training & Awareness:** Records of staff education programs on equitable data practices and awareness campaigns promoting fairness.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires structured policies, measurable\nfairness criteria, and continuous monitoring to ensure training data\nfairly represents all groups the AI system affects.",
  "FA-3": "To comply with **FA-3** **(\"Evaluate system outputs for potential\ndisparities across user groups\")**, look for **systematic evaluation,\ndocumentation, and ongoing monitoring** of fairness in AI outcomes.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Governance:** A formal *Evaluation or Fairness Policy* defining how output disparities are identified, acceptable disparity thresholds, and who is accountable for reviews.\n\n- **Tools & Metrics:** Use of fairness tools (e.g., *Fairlearn*, *AI Fairness 360*, *What-If Tool*) and metrics such as *disparate impact*, *demographic parity*, or *equalized odds* to measure disparities.\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Evidence of scenario testing, demographic segmentation, edge-case analysis, and documented reports highlighting any detected disparities.\n\n- **Monitoring & Improvement:** Ongoing audits, real-time monitoring dashboards, and feedback loops to retrain or adjust models when disparities are found.\n\n- **Documentation & Oversight:** Records of disparity assessments, corrective actions, and oversight by an AI fairness or ethics board.\n\n- **Independent Review:** Third-party audits or fairness certifications validating results and compliance with standards (e.g., *ISO/IEC 24027*).\n\n**In short:** Compliance means consistently analysing and reporting AI\noutputs for unequal impacts, acting on findings, and maintaining\ntransparency and accountability in fairness evaluation processes.",
  "FA-4": "To comply with **FA-4 (\"How do you measure fairness in your AI system's\noutputs?\")**, organizations must **define, quantify, and continuously\nmonitor fairness** using clear metrics and evidence-based evaluation.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Governance:** A *Fairness Measurement Policy* that defines fairness objectives, metrics, and acceptable thresholds for the organization's AI systems.\n\n- **Metrics & Evaluation:** Use of recognised fairness metrics*demographic parity*, *equalized odds*, *predictive parity*, *disparate impact ratio*and, where needed, context-specific custom metrics aligned with ethical principles.\n\n- **Tools & Technology:** Implementation of fairness evaluation platforms (e.g., *Fairlearn*, *AI Fairness 360*, *What-If Tool*, *Responsible AI Toolbox*) and automated systems to track fairness in real time.\n\n- **Testing & Reporting:** Documented fairness testing reports, dashboards visualizing fairness scores, and periodic reviews covering model updates, anomaly alerts, and corrective actions.\n\n- **Stakeholder & Compliance:** Evidence of fairness review boards, staff training, stakeholder feedback, and alignment with *ISO/IEC 24027* or anti-discrimination regulations.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires measurable fairness standards,\ntransparency in evaluation, and continuous monitoring to ensure outputs\nremain equitable, auditable, and aligned with ethical and regulatory\nexpectations.",
  "FA-5": "To comply with **FA-5 (\"Have you tested the system for potential biases\nin real-world scenarios?\")**, look for **evidence of live environment\ntesting, stakeholder input, and bias correction processes** beyond lab\nconditions.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Governance:** A *Real-World Testing Policy* outlining how and when systems are tested in operational contexts, with defined fairness thresholds and approval processes before deployment.\n\n- **Testing Practices:** Documentation of *scenario-based testing*, *pilot deployments*, and *A/B testing* across diverse demographics, geographies, and edge cases to identify bias under real conditions.\n\n- **Results & Analysis:** Reports detailing real-world bias testing methodologies, disparities observed, and metrics used (e.g., *demographic parity*, *equal opportunity*, *disparate impact ratio*).\n\n- **Tools & Adjustments:** Use of fairness and explainability tools (e.g., *Fairlearn*, *AI Fairness 360*, *SHAP*, *LIME*) and evidence of model updates, retraining logs, or changelogs addressing discovered biases.\n\n- **Feedback & Oversight:** Input from users, experts, and governance committees validating findings, with continuous monitoring and periodic bias reports.\n\n- **Independent Review:** Third-party validation or certifications confirming fairness testing integrity.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires systematic real-world bias testing,\ntransparent reporting, and continuous refinement to ensure fairness\nholds beyond controlled development environments.",
  "FA-6": "To comply with **FA-6 (\"Are you using any frameworks or tools to assess\nand mitigate bias?\")**, organizations should show **active, structured\nuse of recognized AI fairness frameworks** integrated into their\ndevelopment and governance processes.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Tools & Frameworks:** Proof of using fairness tools such as *Fairlearn*, *IBM AI Fairness 360*, *Google's What-If Tool*, or *Microsoft's Responsible AI Toolbox* to detect, measure, and mitigate bias in datasets and models.\n\n- **Policies & Integration:** A *Bias Mitigation Policy* or *Fairness Framework* mandating these tools as part of the AI lifecycle, with documented inclusion in development workflows or automated pipelines.\n\n- **Reports & Outputs:** Tool-generated outputs showing detected disparities, mitigation actions, and measurable fairness improvements across demographic groups.\n\n- **Training & Governance:** Evidence that staff are trained to use these tools and that fairness evaluations form a required approval step before deployment.\n\n- **Continuous Monitoring:** Ongoing tool usage for real-time fairness tracking, dashboards, and audit logs showing consistent bias evaluation.\n\n- **External Validation:** Third-party audits or certifications confirming compliance with standards such as *ISO/IEC 24027*.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires demonstrable, ongoing use of trusted\nbias-assessment tools, embedded governance processes, and documented\nimprovements in fairness over time.",
  "FA-7": "To comply with **FA-7 (\"How do you ensure fairness in decision-making\nfor minority or underserved groups?\")**, organizations must show\n**active measures to identify, prevent, and correct bias that could\ndisadvantage vulnerable populations.**\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Governance:** A *Fairness Policy* and *Ethics & Inclusion Guidelines* explicitly protecting minority and underserved groups, backed by oversight from a fairness review board or ethics committee.\n\n- **Data & Model Practices:** Documentation showing diverse and representative datasets, bias audits, and use of techniques such as *oversampling*, *synthetic data generation*, or *threshold adjustments* to improve equity.\n\n- **Metrics & Tools:** Evidence of fairness evaluation using *disparate impact ratio*, *equalized odds*, or *demographic parity*; and tools such as *Fairlearn*, *AI Fairness 360*, *What-If Tool*, or *SHAP/LIME* for explainability.\n\n- **Monitoring & Engagement:** Continuous monitoring for disparities, incident reporting mechanisms, and stakeholder input from affected communities.\n\n- **Training & Compliance:** Staff training on fairness principles, adherence to anti-discrimination laws, and alignment with *ISO/IEC 24027* or ethical AI standards.\n\n**In short:** Compliance means embedding fairness for minority and\nunderserved groups throughout the AI lifecycle  from dataset design\nand testing to governance, transparency, and ongoing accountability.",
  "FA-8": "To comply with **FA-8 (\"Is fairness embedded in your AI model's design\nprocess, or is it addressed post-deployment?\")**, organizations must\nshow that **fairness is built in from the start**, not treated as a\nreactive fix.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Frameworks:** A *Fairness-by-Design Policy* and *End-to-End Fairness Standards* embedding fairness considerations throughout the AI lifecyclefrom data collection to deployment and monitoring.\n\n- **Design Integration:** Documentation of *inclusive design practices*, *fairness checkpoints* during data preparation, model training, and validation, and approval gates requiring fairness reviews before progression.\n\n- **Data & Model Practices:** Evidence of representative datasets, proactive bias detection, and application of bias mitigation techniques (e.g., reweighting, adversarial debiasing).\n\n- **Tools & Oversight:** Use of fairness and explainability tools (*Fairlearn*, *AI Fairness 360*, *Responsible AI Toolbox*, *SHAP*, *LIME*) and oversight committees enforcing fairness compliance.\n\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Real-time fairness monitoring, incident management logs, and documented audits or reports showing continuous improvement.\n\n- **Training & Engagement:** Evidence of design team training, ethics workshops, and stakeholder involvement in shaping fairness standards.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires demonstrating a *fairness-by-design\napproach*integrating equity, transparency, and accountability into\nevery phase of AI system development and beyond.",
  "TR-1": "To comply with **TR-1 (\"What level of detail about the AI system's\nfunctionality and limitations is communicated to users or\nstakeholders?\")**, organizations should demonstrate **clear, accessible,\nand audience-appropriate transparency** around what their AI can and\ncannot do.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Transparency Policy* and *Communication Guidelines* defining what information must be shared with users and stakeholders, including known limitations and risks.\n\n- **Documentation:** Detailed technical documentation for internal audiences and simplified user-facing materials (manuals, FAQs, visuals) that explain AI functionality, boundaries, and potential biases.\n\n- **Explainability & Tools:** Use of interpretability tools (*SHAP*, *LIME*) or interactive dashboards that help visualize AI decision factors and limitations.\n\n- **Stakeholder Engagement:** Records of workshops, feedback sessions, or updates showing that stakeholders were informed about system performance and constraints.\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Usability tests proving users understood system capabilities and limitations, with results used to improve clarity.\n\n- **Compliance:** Evidence that disclosures align with legal or ethical standards (e.g., GDPR \"right to explanation\", ISO/IEC AI standards).\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires proactively communicating the AI\nsystem's purpose, strengths, and limitations in clear, audience-relevant\nlanguage to foster transparency, trust, and accountability.",
  "TR-2": "To comply with **TR-2 (\"How do you document the data sources,\nalgorithms, and processes used in your AI system?\")**, organizations\nshould maintain **comprehensive, version-controlled documentation**\ncovering all key components of their AI systems.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Documentation Policy* defining required detail for data sources, algorithms, and processes, and *Transparency Standards* guiding documentation depth for different audiences.\n\n- **Data Records:** Metadata describing data origin, collection methods, preprocessing steps, and demographic representativeness, with dataset version histories and audit logs.\n\n- **Algorithm Documentation:** Clear explanations of algorithm purpose, logic, and parameters; flowcharts illustrating decision pathways; and version histories with testing results and risk assessments.\n\n- **Process Documentation:** Records of lifecycle stages (design → testing → deployment), key decision logs, and workflow descriptions showing how AI integrates into business operations.\n\n- **Tools & Governance:** Use of platforms like *Git*, *Confluence*, or *Jupyter Notebooks* for documentation; oversight from governance bodies and audit trails verifying compliance.\n\n- **Accessibility & Updates:** Centralized storage, accessible to relevant teams, with change logs and continuous improvement records.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires structured, auditable documentation\nthat enables traceability, accountability, and transparency across all\nAI data, algorithm, and process elements.",
  "TR-3": "To comply with **TR-3 (\"Are there mechanisms for external parties to\nunderstand how your AI system works?\")**, organizations must show that\n**regulators, customers, and other stakeholders have clear, structured\naccess** to information explaining their AI system's purpose, design,\nand limitations.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Transparency Policy* and *Stakeholder Communication Guidelines* defining what information is shared externally, how often, and through what channels.\n\n- **External-Facing Documentation:** User guides, FAQs, or *Transparency Reports* explaining the AI system's data use, logic, and limitations in plain language.\n\n- **Access Mechanisms:** Public dashboards, secure portals, or APIs that allow stakeholders to view summaries or explore decision logic through interpretable visualizations (e.g., via *SHAP*, *LIME*).\n\n- **Governance & Validation:** Evidence that communication tools are approved by oversight bodies, tested with regulators or customers for clarity, and updated based on feedback.\n\n- **Third-Party Assurance:** External audits or certifications validating that transparency mechanisms meet industry or regulatory standards (e.g., *GDPR*, *ISO/IEC 23053*).\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires user-friendly, verifiable mechanisms\nfor external parties to understand and trust your AI system's\nfunctionality, promoting accountability and ethical transparency.",
  "TR-4": "To comply with **TR-4 (\"How is information about your AI system shared\nwith internal teams and external stakeholders?\")**, organizations must\nshow **structured, audience-appropriate communication processes**\nensuring everyone understands how the AI system works.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** An *Information Sharing Policy* and *Transparency Guidelines* defining what information is shared, with whom, and through which channels.\n\n- **Internal Communication:** Centralized documentation (e.g., *Confluence*, *SharePoint*), training materials, and cross-functional meetings to align technical, compliance, and business teams.\n\n- **External Communication:** Tailored materials for stakeholderstechnical reports for regulators, non-technical FAQs or infographics for customers, and dashboards showing system metrics and transparency updates.\n\n- **Governance & Access Control:** Approval records from governance committees, audit logs confirming compliant sharing, and measures ensuring sensitive information is securely shared.\n\n- **Feedback & Engagement:** Evidence of stakeholder engagement sessions, feedback loops, and change logs showing updates following system improvements or stakeholder input.\n\n- **Compliance:** Documentation proving adherence to relevant transparency and data regulations (*GDPR*, *ISO/IEC 23053*).\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires effective, secure, and well-governed\ninformation sharing that keeps both internal teams and external\nstakeholders informed, aligned, and confident in the AI system's\ntransparency.",
  "TR-5": "To comply with **TR-5 (\"Are there any 'black-box' components in your\nsystem that could impede transparency?\")**, organizations must identify\nopaque AI components and demonstrate **active measures to explain,\nmonitor, and mitigate their impact** on transparency.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Transparency Policy* and *Explainability Standards* defining how black-box components are identified, evaluated, and managed.\n\n- **System Documentation:** Architecture diagrams and component assessments highlighting any opaque models (e.g., deep learning networks) and explaining associated transparency risks.\n\n- **Mitigation Measures:** Use of explainability tools (*SHAP*, *LIME*, counterfactual analysis), surrogate interpretable models, or human oversight processes to validate AI decisions.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Records from review committees approving black-box model use, with documented justifications, decision logs, and transparency risk assessments.\n\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Transparency dashboards, incident logs, and regular audits tracking interpretability levels and corrective actions.\n\n- **Training & Communication:** Staff training on explainability techniques and stakeholder communications disclosing black-box limitations and mitigation strategies.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires recognizing where black-box models\nexist, transparently managing their risks, and demonstrating a\nstructured approach to making complex AI systems understandable and\naccountable.",
  "TR-6": "To comply with **TR-6 (\"How do you disclose the sources of data and\nalgorithms used in the AI system?\")**, organizations must demonstrate\n**structured, transparent disclosure practices** that explain where data\noriginates and how algorithms are developed or sourced.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Disclosure Policy* and *Transparency Guidelines* defining what information must be shared, the level of detail required, and how frequently it's updated.\n\n- **Data Source Disclosure:** Comprehensive records of all data sources (internal, third-party, open datasets), including origin, licensing, preprocessing steps, and identified limitations or biases.\n\n- **Algorithm Disclosure:** Documentation describing algorithm purpose, functionality, parameters, and risksplus visual flowcharts or explainability outputs (e.g., *SHAP*, *LIME*) for clarity.\n\n- **Communication & Accessibility:** Regularly published *Transparency Reports*, stakeholder-specific summaries, or interactive dashboards giving access to data and algorithm details.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Approval logs from governance committees, audit records confirming accuracy, and stakeholder feedback mechanisms.\n\n- **Compliance & Training:** Evidence of adherence to *GDPR Articles 13--14*, *ISO/IEC 23053*, and internal staff training on disclosure requirements.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires clear, verifiable, and accessible\ndisclosures about data and algorithmsensuring transparency,\naccountability, and trust with all stakeholders.",
  "TR-7": "To comply with **TR-7 (\"Are users made aware when they are interacting\nwith an AI system?\")**, organizations must show that **users are\nclearly, consistently, and accessibly informed** when engaging with\nAI-driven systems.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Transparency Policy* and *User Awareness Standards* detailing how and when AI interaction disclosures are made.\n\n- **Notification Mechanisms:** Visible, upfront labels (\"AI Assistant,\" \"Powered by AI\"), pop-ups, or banners in interfaces; statements in *Terms of Service* or *Privacy Policies*; and icons or badges denoting AI involvement.\n\n- **User Education:** FAQs, onboarding messages, or guides explaining AI roles and limitations, translated and accessible across languages and formats.\n\n- **Governance & Testing:** Approval from internal governance or legal teams, audit logs confirming consistent implementation, and usability testing verifying that users understand AI disclosures.\n\n- **Accessibility & Compliance:** Adherence to *WCAG accessibility standards* and legal requirements such as *GDPR Article 22* (automated decision-making transparency).\n\n- **Feedback & Continuous Improvement:** Systems for collecting user feedback, incident logs for missed disclosures, and updates ensuring ongoing compliance.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires explicit, accessible, and well-tested\nnotifications ensuring users always know when they're interacting with\nAIsupporting transparency, accountability, and user trust.",
  "TR-8": "To comply with **TR-8 (\"Do you publish any transparency reports or\ndocumentation for regulators or customers?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate a **consistent, structured approach to publishing AI\ntransparency reports** that communicate system performance, risks, and\ngovernance to stakeholders.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Transparency Reporting Policy* and *Publishing Standards* defining content scope, frequency (e.g., annual/quarterly), and review procedures for reports.\n\n- **Transparency Reports:** Published documents detailing the AI system's purpose, data sources, algorithms, performance metrics, risks, mitigation steps, and incident handling. Reports may be tailored for different audiencestechnical for regulators, plain-language summaries for customers.\n\n- **Accessibility & Governance:** Reports made available via public websites or dashboards, reviewed and approved by governance or legal teams, and redacted to protect proprietary information.\n\n- **Monitoring & Updates:** Logs showing regular updates following system changes, audit reports validating accuracy, and compliance with *GDPR* or *ISO/IEC 23053* standards.\n\n- **Engagement & Feedback:** Evidence of stakeholder communication, feedback loops, and improvements made based on external input.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires regularly published, accessible\ntransparency reports that demonstrate accountability, build trust, and\nprovide clear visibility into AI systems for both regulators and\ncustomers.",
  "EX-1": "To comply with **EX-1 (\"How do you decide the level of explainability\nrequired for your AI system?\")**, organizations must show a\n**structured, risk-based process** for determining how much and what\nkind of explanation stakeholders need.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Criteria:** A formal *Explainability Policy* defining how explainability levels are set, including decision factors such as system complexity, regulatory demands, and ethical considerations.\n\n- **Risk & Impact Assessment:** Documentation showing evaluation of potential harm, decision sensitivity (e.g., financial, medical), and system scope to determine the depth of explanation needed.\n\n- **Stakeholder Analysis:** Records showing engagement with end-users, regulators, and internal teams to identify their specific explainability requirements.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Evidence that explainability decisions are reviewed and approved by AI oversight or ethics committees, with logs documenting chosen explainability levels and rationale.\n\n- **Tools & Methodologies:** Use of frameworks or explainability tools (e.g., *SHAP*, *LIME*) integrated during design, with documentation of trade-offs between performance and interpretability.\n\n- **Monitoring & Review:** Records of periodic reassessments based on regulatory changes or stakeholder feedback.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires a documented, risk- and\nstakeholder-driven approach ensuring that AI explanations are\nproportionate, transparent, and aligned with ethical and regulatory\nexpectations.",
  "EX-2": "To comply with **EX-2 (\"What methods are used to test whether\nexplanations are understandable by non-technical users?\")**,\norganizations must show that **AI explanations are tested for clarity,\naccessibility, and real-world comprehension** by diverse non-technical\naudiences.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** *Explainability Standards* and *User-Centric Design Guidelines* defining clarity criteria and ensuring explanations suit non-technical users.\n\n- **Testing & Evaluation:** Records of usability tests, pilot programs, or focus groups measuring comprehension scores, satisfaction, and time to understand explanations.\n\n- **Feedback Mechanisms:** Surveys or interviews capturing user feedback on clarity, with results used to improve explanations iteratively.\n\n- **Visualization & Tools:** Use of visual aids (e.g., flowcharts, dashboards) and explainability frameworks (*SHAP*, *LIME*, counterfactuals) simplified for non-technical interpretation.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Approval records and audit results confirming explanations meet clarity and comprehension standards.\n\n- **Accessibility & Inclusion:** Language localization, assistive features (e.g., screen reader compatibility), and inclusive testing with varied user demographics.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Feedback analysis, comprehension metrics, and documented improvements based on testing outcomes.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires structured user testing, stakeholder\nfeedback, and iterative refinements ensuring AI explanations are\ngenuinely understandable by non-technical users.",
  "EX-3": "To comply with **EX-3 (\"Do you provide explanations in different formats\nfor different audiences?\")**, organizations must show that **AI\nexplanations are tailored to the needs, expertise, and preferences of\ndiverse stakeholders** through multiple, accessible formats.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** An *Explainability Policy* and *Audience-Specific Standards* defining how explanations are adapted for technical, non-technical, and regulatory audiences.\n\n- **Documentation & Resources:** Audience profiles or personas identifying stakeholder needs, supported by repositories of explanation templates (visual, textual, technical).\n\n- **Explanation Formats:**\n\n- *Visualizations* (e.g., decision trees, graphs, interactive\ndashboards).\n\n- *Text Summaries* (plain-language overviews for non-technical users).\n\n- *Technical Reports* (detailed algorithm documentation and metrics).\n\n- *Multimedia* (videos, animations, tutorials).\n\n- **Stakeholder Testing & Feedback:** Usability studies, surveys, and workshops validating clarity and effectiveness across audiences.\n\n- **Tools & Frameworks:** Use of *SHAP*, *LIME*, or visualization tools like *Tableau* or *Power BI* to generate tailored outputs.\n\n- **Accessibility & Governance:** Multi-language support, accessibility features, and governance reviews ensuring compliance with *GDPR* and *ISO/IEC explainability standards*.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires offering clear, inclusive, and\ncontext-appropriate AI explanationsso every audience can understand\nthe system's reasoning, regardless of expertise level.",
  "EX-4": "To comply with **EX-4 (\"How do you evaluate the accuracy and\ncompleteness of the explanations provided?\")**, organizations must show\nthat **AI explanations are validated to accurately reflect system\nbehaviour and include all relevant decision factors.**\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** An *Evaluation Policy* defining how explanation quality is assessed, with criteria covering accuracy, completeness, and regulatory alignment.\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Evidence of cross-checking explanations against model logic, outputs, and input data; simulations ensuring explanations remain correct across scenarios.\n\n- **Stakeholder Feedback:** Surveys, interviews, or feedback logs from users, technical teams, and regulators confirming explanations are understandable and accurate.\n\n- **Tools & Metrics:** Use of *SHAP*, *LIME*, or error analysis frameworks to verify fidelity between model outputs and explanations; performance metrics such as comprehension rates and fidelity scores.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Review and approval records from governance or ethics boards; audit reports verifying explanation quality and completeness.\n\n- **Monitoring & Continuous Improvement:** Logs showing updates to explanations as systems evolve or new issues arise.\n\n- **Compliance:** Evidence of adherence to *GDPR Article 22* and *ISO/IEC explainability standards*.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires a repeatable, measurable process\nensuring AI explanations are accurate, complete, and aligned with both\nsystem logic and stakeholder expectations.",
  "EX-5": "To comply with **EX-5 (\"Are there trade-offs between model complexity\nand explainability in your system design?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate a **structured, transparent approach to balancing\nperformance with interpretability** throughout AI system development.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** *System Design Policies* and *Trade-Off Guidelines* defining how explainability is prioritizedespecially in high-stakes or regulated applications.\n\n- **Design Documentation:** Trade-off analyses comparing complex (e.g., deep learning) vs. interpretable (e.g., decision tree) models, with clear justifications for chosen architectures.\n\n- **Governance Oversight:** Evidence of governance or ethics committee reviews approving trade-off decisions, with recorded rationales in meeting logs.\n\n- **Testing & Evaluation:** Reports comparing performance gains from complexity versus explainability reductions; user comprehension testing of model outputs.\n\n- **Tools & Techniques:** Use of *SHAP*, *LIME*, or surrogate models to enhance interpretability; adoption of hybrid model approaches for balance.\n\n- **Stakeholder Engagement:** Records showing stakeholder consultation and feedback on preferred trade-offs between accuracy and transparency.\n\n- **Compliance & Ethics:** Adherence to *GDPR Article 22* and *ISO/IEC 23053* documentation standards for explainable AI.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires evidence that model design decisions\ncarefully weigh performance against transparency, supported by\ngovernance review, stakeholder input, and ethical accountability.",
  "EX-6": "To comply with **EX-6 (\"How do you ensure the decisions made by your AI\nsystem are interpretable by users?\")**, organizations must show that\n**AI decisions are designed, tested, and communicated in ways that users\ncan clearly understand and trust.**\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** An *Interpretability Policy* and *Explainability Guidelines* defining acceptable interpretability levels for different audiences (technical, non-technical, external).\n\n- **Design & Implementation:** Evidence that interpretability influenced model selection (e.g., preference for decision trees or linear models), supported by explainability tools (*SHAP*, *LIME*, counterfactuals) and surrogate models for complex systems.\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Usability tests, comprehension assessments, and metrics showing users correctly understand AI decisions and explanations.\n\n- **User Communication & Feedback:** Clear, user-facing explanationsvisual aids, text summaries, or dashboardsplus feedback mechanisms driving continuous improvement.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Review and approval of interpretability measures by governance bodies, with periodic audits confirming compliance.\n\n- **Compliance & Training:** Alignment with *GDPR* (right to explanation) and *ISO/IEC 23053* standards, supported by staff training on interpretability practices.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires structured, user-tested, and\ntransparent design choices ensuring that AI decisions are both\ninterpretable and trustworthy for all stakeholders.",
  "EX-7": "To comply with **EX-7 (\"What tools or techniques do you use to make\ncomplex models more explainable?\")**, organizations must show that\n**they use formal explainability toolssuch as SHAP, LIME, or\nequivalentsto interpret and communicate how complex AI models make\ndecisions.**\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Tools & Techniques:** Documentation confirming the use of *SHAP*, *LIME*, *Alibi*, *ELI5*, or custom visualization tools, including rationale for their selection and application scope.\n\n- **Integration & Workflow:** Evidence that explainability tools are built into the model development, validation, and deployment pipelines, not just used post-hoc.\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Reports verifying that explanation outputs align with model behavior, alongside stakeholder testing for clarity and accuracy.\n\n- **Training & Awareness:** Records showing staff training on explainability tools and access to tool-specific guides or tutorials.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Approvals from AI or data science governance bodies reviewing the effectiveness of explainability tools.\n\n- **Stakeholder Communication:** Visual dashboards, feature importance graphs, and plain-language summaries tailored for technical and non-technical audiences.\n\n- **Compliance:** Alignment with *GDPR* and *ISO/IEC 23053* explainability requirements.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires consistent, validated, and transparent\nuse of recognized explainability tools to bridge the gap between complex\nmodels and human understanding.",
  "EX-8": "To comply with **EX-8 (\"Are there specific groups for whom explanations\nof AI outputs are tailored?\")**, organizations must show that **AI\nexplanations are customized for distinct audiences**such as\nend-users, regulators, and internal teamsbased on their expertise,\nneeds, and roles.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** A formal *Explainability Policy* and *Audience-Specific Standards* defining how explanations differ by group (e.g., level of technical detail, regulatory depth).\n\n- **Stakeholder Analysis:** User profiles or personas documenting each group's expectations, expertise, and preferred explanation formats.\n\n- **Tailored Mechanisms:** Concrete examplesplain-language explanations or visuals for end-users, detailed compliance reports for regulators, and technical documentation for internal teams.\n\n- **Testing & Feedback:** Usability testing, focus groups, or surveys confirming explanations meet each audience's comprehension level and purpose.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Review and approval records verifying that tailoring aligns with governance and regulatory requirements.\n\n- **Accessibility & Inclusivity:** Evidence of multi-language support, accessibility features, and adaptations for diverse or disabled users.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Feedback loops and metrics (e.g., comprehension or satisfaction scores) guiding iterative refinement.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires structured, user-centred\nexplainability practices ensuring each stakeholder group receives clear,\nrelevant, and accessible AI explanations.",
  "AC-1": "To comply with **AC-1 (\"How is accountability for AI system failures\nmanaged within your organization?\")**, organizations must show that\n**clear ownership, structured processes, and governance mechanisms are\nin place to manage AI failures effectively.**\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Frameworks:** A formal *Accountability Policy* and *Incident Management Framework* defining how AI failures are identified, escalated, and resolved, with specific accountability assignments.\n\n- **Roles & Responsibilities:** Documented role descriptions for AI System Owners, Risk Managers, and Incident Response Teams, including clear escalation paths and decision-making authority.\n\n- **Incident Management:** Evidence of *incident response plans*, *incident logs*, and *post-incident analyses* outlining the nature, impact, and resolution of failures.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Minutes or approvals from ethics or oversight committees verifying accountability processes are regularly reviewed.\n\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Systems to detect failures, track performance, and report incidents internally and externally.\n\n- **Stakeholder Engagement:** Communication protocols and feedback mechanisms ensuring transparency with affected users or regulators.\n\n- **Training & Awareness:** Employee training on incident response and accountability expectations.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned, root-cause analyses, and updates to accountability policies.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires demonstrable ownership, structured\nresponse processes, and transparent reporting to ensure responsible\nmanagement of AI failures.",
  "AC-2": "To comply with **AC-2 (\"What governance structures are in place to\nmonitor AI outcomes?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that **formal\noversight mechanisms and governance frameworks** exist to monitor,\nreview, and guide AI system outcomes across ethical, legal, and\noperational dimensions.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Governance Framework:** A documented *AI Governance Framework* outlining roles, responsibilities, and authority of governance structures (e.g., AI Oversight Board, Ethics Committee, Risk Team).\n\n- **Policies:** *Monitoring and Reporting Policies* specifying how AI performance, fairness, and compliance are tracked and reviewed.\n\n- **Governance Structures:** Organizational charts, committee charters, and meeting records showing regular oversight of AI systems.\n\n- **Decision-Making Processes:** Minutes and approval logs from governance body meetings evidencing active review and intervention.\n\n- **Oversight Mechanisms:** Use of dashboards, audits, or monitoring tools to assess AI performance and detect anomalies.\n\n- **Risk Management:** Risk registers and mitigation plans maintained and reviewed by governance teams.\n\n- **Accountability & Reporting:** Annual reports, post-incident oversight, and escalation procedures.\n\n- **Regulatory & Ethical Compliance:** Proof of alignment with frameworks such as GDPR and ISO/IEC AI governance standards.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Evidence of periodic reviews, training, and updates to governance processes.\n\n**In short:** Effective AI governance requires structured oversight,\ntransparent decision-making, and continuous monitoring to ensure safe,\nethical, and compliant AI outcomes.",
  "AC-3": "To comply with **AC-3 (\"Are there policies for escalating issues\nidentified in the AI system?\")**, organizations must show that **formal\nescalation procedures exist** to ensure timely and appropriate handling\nof AI-related incidents, especially those with ethical, regulatory, or\noperational implications.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Escalation Policy:** A documented policy defining what issues require escalation (e.g., critical failures, bias, security incidents) and outlining how they progress through the organization.\n\n- **Roles & Responsibilities:** Clear role definitions and escalation pathways involving AI System Owners, Risk Managers, and Incident Response Leads.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Records showing oversight bodies (e.g., AI Governance Boards, Ethics Committees) review escalated issues and approve resolutions.\n\n- **Detection & Reporting:** Use of monitoring tools and issue reporting mechanisms (e.g., ticketing systems, anonymous reporting).\n\n- **Escalation Triggers:** Defined severity levels and thresholds determining when and how issues are escalated.\n\n- **Communication Protocols:** Notification plans for internal stakeholders, executives, and regulators.\n\n- **Training & Drills:** Evidence of employee training and simulations to test escalation readiness.\n\n- **Continuous Review:** Logs, feedback loops, and audits verifying escalation effectiveness and regulatory compliance.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires structured, documented escalation\npolicies ensuring critical AI issues are identified, communicated, and\nresolved efficiently with full governance oversight.",
  "AC-4": "To comply with **AC-4 (\"How do you handle customer complaints or\ndisputes related to AI outputs?\")**, organizations must show that\n**clear, fair, and transparent complaint-handling processes** exist to\naddress customer disputes about AI decisions.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A formal *Complaint Handling Policy* and *Dispute Resolution Procedure* detailing how AI-related complaints are received, investigated, resolved, and communicated.\n\n- **Accessible Channels:** Multiple customer-friendly options for submitting complaints (e.g., online forms, helpdesk, chatbot, or email) with multilingual and accessible design.\n\n- **Roles & Responsibilities:** Designated teams or officers (e.g., AI Ethics Officer, Risk Manager, Customer Support) responsible for reviewing and escalating complaints.\n\n- **Tracking & Analysis:** Complaint logs and systems (e.g., Zendesk, Salesforce) recording issue details, timelines, root-cause analysis, and resolution outcomes.\n\n- **Governance Oversight:** Ethics or AI oversight committees reviewing recurring or high-impact disputes.\n\n- **Transparency:** Evidence of communications with customers confirming receipt, updates, and outcomes of complaints.\n\n- **Regulatory & Ethical Compliance:** Adherence to consumer protection laws, GDPR, and ethical principles like fairness and non-discrimination.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Trend reports, lessons learned, and updates to procedures based on feedback.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires documented, transparent, and inclusive\ncomplaint-handling processes ensuring fairness, accountability, and\ncustomer trust in AI outcomes.",
  "AC-5": "To comply with **AC-5 (\"Are roles and responsibilities for AI governance\nclearly defined across teams?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that\n**accountability for AI governance is formalized, documented, and\nwell-communicated** across all relevant departments.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Governance Framework:** A documented AI Governance Framework outlining roles and responsibilities across technical, legal, risk, and compliance teams.\n\n- **Role Descriptions:** Clearly defined job or team descriptions specifying accountability for AI design, deployment, monitoring, and compliance oversight.\n\n- **Organizational Structure:** Organizational charts and committee records (e.g., AI Ethics Board, Risk Committee) showing reporting lines and governance ownership.\n\n- **Accountability Matrix:** Use of a RACI or similar tool identifying who is *Responsible, Accountable, Consulted,* and *Informed* for each governance activity.\n\n- **Communication Protocols:** Evidence of structured inter-team communication (meeting records, collaboration procedures).\n\n- **Training & Awareness:** Programs educating employees on their AI governance roles and duties.\n\n- **Governance Oversight:** Periodic audits or reviews assessing role clarity and effectiveness.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Updates to frameworks or policies reflecting lessons learned and evolving regulations.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **clear, organization-wide\ndefinition and communication of AI governance roles**supported by\npolicies, documentation, training, and regular reviewsto ensure\nconsistent accountability and effective oversight.",
  "AC-6": "To comply with **AC-6 (\"Who is responsible for monitoring and managing\nthe AI system throughout its lifecycle?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate that **clear ownership and structured processes exist for\ncontinuous oversight of AI systems** from design through\ndecommissioning.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Lifecycle Management Policy:** Formal documentation defining roles, responsibilities, and procedures for monitoring, updating, and retiring AI systems.\n\n- **Named Roles:** Clearly identified **AI System Owners**, **Monitoring Leads**, and **Incident Response Teams**, supported by RACI matrices assigning accountability across lifecycle stages.\n\n- **Governance Oversight:** Evidence of AI Ethics Boards or Risk Committees periodically reviewing system performance, risks, and compliance status.\n\n- **Monitoring & Maintenance:** Use of dashboards and automated tools to track performance metrics, model drift, bias, or anomalies; regular maintenance and patching schedules.\n\n- **Incident Management:** Defined escalation and resolution workflows with detailed incident logs.\n\n- **Training & Resources:** Role-specific training and guides for staff managing monitoring and compliance activities.\n\n- **Reporting & Feedback:** Established reporting lines to senior management and feedback loops for ongoing improvement.\n\n- **Compliance & Audits:** Documentation and audit findings demonstrating adherence to lifecycle monitoring requirements (e.g., ISO/IEC AI standards).\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **formal, role-based framework\nensuring continuous, accountable management** of AI systems throughout\ntheir entire operational lifecycle.",
  "AC-7": "To comply with **AC-7 (\"Are there audit trails or logs that document the\nAI system's decision-making process?\")**, organizations must show that\n**comprehensive, secure, and reviewable logging mechanisms** exist to\nrecord how AI decisions are made and maintained over time.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Logging Policy:** A formal policy detailing how decision-making data (inputs, model logic, outputs, timestamps) is logged, secured, and retained.\n\n- **Technical Logs:** System-generated logs capturing input data, decision steps, model parameters, and output results for traceability.\n\n- **Governance Oversight:** Regular reviews of logs by AI governance or risk teams to ensure accountability and compliance.\n\n- **Integrity & Access Controls:** Role-based permissions, audit logs of access activity, and integrity checks to prevent tampering.\n\n- **Incident Response:** Examples showing logs used to investigate and resolve issues, validate model outcomes, or respond to customer/regulator inquiries.\n\n- **Regulatory & Ethical Compliance:** Evidence of adherence to GDPR and ISO/IEC standards on traceability and automated decision transparency.\n\n- **Tools & Systems:** Use of logging frameworks (e.g., Splunk, ELK Stack) and integration with explainability tools like SHAP or LIME for enhanced interpretability.\n\n- **Training & Reporting:** Staff trained on interpreting logs; periodic reports generated from logged data for management and compliance.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **secure, traceable audit trails**\nthat make every AI decision explainable, verifiable, and accountable.",
  "AC-8": "To comply with **AC-8 (\"What mechanisms are in place to address disputes\nor concerns about the system's outcomes?\")**, organizations must show\nthat **clear, fair, and accessible processes exist for users and\nstakeholders to raise, investigate, and resolve concerns** about\nAI-generated decisions.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Dispute Resolution Policy:** A formal policy defining the types of disputes covered (e.g., incorrect results, bias, ethical concerns) and how they are escalated.\n\n- **Accessible Channels:** User-friendly submission options such as web forms, helpdesk tickets, or dedicated emails/chatbots, with options for anonymity.\n\n- **Roles & Accountability:** Clearly defined responsibilities (e.g., AI Ethics Officers, System Owners) supported by a RACI matrix and oversight by ethics or risk committees.\n\n- **Investigation & Analysis:** Logs and root-cause reports showing how disputes were reviewed, including bias and fairness assessments using explainability tools like SHAP or LIME.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Evidence of regular dispute reviews by oversight boards and corrective action tracking.\n\n- **Reporting & Feedback:** Metrics on dispute volume, types, and resolution times; stakeholder feedback loops.\n\n- **Training & Awareness:** Staff trained in dispute handling, fairness, and stakeholder communication.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Policy updates and lessons-learned reports feeding back into AI system and governance enhancements.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **a transparent, accessible, and\nwell-governed dispute resolution framework** that builds trust and\nensures fairness in AI outcomes.",
  "DI-1": "To comply with **DI-1 (\"How do you validate the accuracy, completeness,\nand quality of the data used in your AI systems?\")**, organizations must\nshow that **data integrity is actively monitored and validated**\nthroughout the AI lifecycle to ensure reliable, bias-free, and compliant\noutcomes.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Data Quality Policy:** Formal documentation defining accuracy, completeness, and quality metrics (e.g., error rates, duplication rates) and validation procedures.\n\n- **Validation Framework:** A structured approach combining automated checks and human review to assess data integrity at collection, preprocessing, and modeling stages.\n\n- **Validation Processes:** Reports or logs from completeness, accuracy, and consistency tests using tools like *Great Expectations*, *Talend*, or *OpenRefine*.\n\n- **Data Governance:** Clearly defined roles (data stewards, governance committees) and systems for **data lineage tracking** and stewardship accountability.\n\n- **Monitoring & Dashboards:** Real-time data quality dashboards tracking trends, anomalies, and remediation actions.\n\n- **Audit & Review:** Internal and third-party audit findings verifying adherence to data quality standards (e.g., ISO/IEC 27001, GDPR).\n\n- **Training & Awareness:** Staff training in data validation techniques and ethical handling.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Root-cause analyses and process updates to correct recurring issues.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **systematic, auditable data\nvalidation program** ensuring all AI inputs remain accurate, complete,\nand trustworthy.",
  "DI-2": "To comply with **DI-2 (\"What processes do you have for managing data\nlineage and tracking changes to data over time?\")**, organizations must\nshow that **data origins, transformations, and changes are continuously\ntracked, documented, and governed** to ensure transparency,\ntraceability, and regulatory compliance.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Data Lineage Policy:** Formal policy defining how data sources, transformations, and destinations are identified and recorded, including scope, terminology, and update frequency.\n\n- **Change Management Policy:** Documented processes for recording, approving, and version-controlling changes to data, schemas, or pipelines.\n\n- **Lineage Tools:** Use of automated lineage and cataloguing tools (*Collibra*, *Informatica*, *Alation*, *Talend*) or dataset version control (*Git*, *DVC*) to visualize and trace data flow.\n\n- **Governance & Stewardship:** Assigned data stewards responsible for maintaining lineage accuracy and monitoring data modifications.\n\n- **Documentation & Metadata:** Lineage diagrams, transformation logs, and metadata records detailing data origins, movements, and changes.\n\n- **Change & Audit Logs:** Version-controlled logs recording what was changed, when, by whom, and whysupported by monitoring dashboards and integrity reports.\n\n- **Compliance Evidence:** Alignment with GDPR, SOX, ISO/IEC, or sector-specific traceability standards.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Regular reviews, root-cause analyses, and updates to lineage policies and tools based on audit findings.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **end-to-end data traceability and\nchange control** across the entire AI data lifecycle.",
  "DI-3": "To comply with **DI-3 (\"Are there governance policies for data\ncollection, usage, retention, and disposal?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate that **data is ethically, legally, and securely managed\nacross its entire lifecycle**  from acquisition to destruction.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Comprehensive Data Governance Policy:** A formal policy outlining procedures for **data collection, usage, retention, and disposal**, incorporating regulatory and ethical requirements.\n\n- **Collection:** Defines lawful and minimal data gathering, consent mechanisms, and verification of data sources.\n\n- **Usage:** Establishes approved purposes, access controls, and audit trails to prevent misuse.\n\n- **Retention:** Specifies justified retention periods aligned with business and legal requirements, with periodic reviews.\n\n- **Disposal:** Details secure deletion, destruction, or anonymization processes, with disposal logs and third-party validation where applicable.\n\n- **Compliance Alignment:** Evidence of adherence to frameworks like **GDPR**, **Australian Privacy Act**, **HIPAA**, or **PCI DSS**, and ethical principles such as fairness and privacy.\n\n- **Governance Roles:** Appointment of data protection officers or stewards accountable for enforcing lifecycle compliance.\n\n- **Monitoring, Training, and Auditing:** Systems for continuous policy monitoring, staff awareness programs, and regular internal/external audits.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Updates to governance policies based on audit results, incidents, or regulatory changes.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **documented, enforceable lifecycle\npolicies** ensuring lawful, ethical, and secure data management.",
  "DI-4": "To comply with **DI-4 (\"How do you ensure the data used in your AI\nsystems is up-to-date and accurate?\")**, organizations must show that\n**data freshness and accuracy are proactively maintained** through\nautomated, procedural, and governance-based controls.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Data Accuracy & Update Policy:** Formal documentation outlining verification steps, update schedules, quality thresholds, and approval workflows for data changes.\n\n- **Validation & Verification:** Regular data quality checks (e.g., accuracy, consistency, anomaly detection) and third-party verification against trusted sources.\n\n- **Update Mechanisms:** Automated data refresh systems (APIs, cron jobs, or streaming pipelines) and manual update procedures with review checkpoints.\n\n- **Roles & Responsibilities:** Designated data stewards or owners accountable for accuracy, update frequency, and issue escalation.\n\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Real-time dashboards and alerts for stale or inaccurate data, along with periodic data quality performance reports.\n\n- **Testing & Impact Analysis:** Logs showing pre-deployment validation of updated datasets and reports evaluating their effect on AI model outputs.\n\n- **Audit & Continuous Improvement:** Regular audits, root-cause analyses, and policy refinements following data quality incidents.\n\n- **Compliance & Ethics:** Evidence of adherence to data quality standards under frameworks like **GDPR**, **ISO/IEC**, or **Australian Privacy Act**.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **continuous data validation,\nautomation, and accountability** to ensure AI decisions remain reliable\nand current.",
  "DI-5": "To comply with **DI-5 (\"What processes are in place to address missing\nor incomplete data?\")**, organizations must demonstrate **systematic,\ndocumented methods for detecting, resolving, and preventing incomplete\ndatasets** that could impair AI accuracy or fairness.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Data Quality Policy:** Formal guidance on identifying and resolving missing data, including defined completeness thresholds and approved remediation techniques.\n\n- **Validation & Monitoring:** Automated checks (e.g., null value detection, completeness scoring) and dashboards that track and visualize data coverage gaps.\n\n- **Remediation Processes:**\n\n- **Imputation:** Applying statistical (mean/median/mode), predictive, or time-series imputation methods.\n\n- **Collection Enhancements:** Improving input validation, form design, or data entry controls to prevent gaps.\n\n- **Manual Reviews:** Documented correction of missing data with change tracking.\n\n- **Governance & Roles:** Designated data stewards or quality teams accountable for monitoring completeness and resolving issues.\n\n- **Reporting & Auditing:** Regular completeness reports, incident logs, and audits verifying adherence to completeness standards.\n\n- **Testing & Impact Analysis:** Evidence showing how missing data affects model performance and steps taken to mitigate bias or inaccuracy.\n\n- **Tools & Automation:** Integration of data cleaning and validation tools (e.g., *Great Expectations*, *OpenRefine*, *TensorFlow Data Validation*) into AI pipelines.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proactive detection, remediation,\nand prevention** of missing data through policies, automation, and\ngovernance.",
  "DI-6": "To comply with **DI-6 (\"How do you manage data preprocessing, e.g.,\ncleaning and normalization?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that\n**data is systematically cleaned, normalized, and transformed** before\nbeing used in AI models to ensure accuracy, consistency, and\nreliability.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Data Preprocessing Policy:** A formal policy and SOPs detailing standardized methods for cleaning (duplicate removal, outlier handling), normalization (scaling, encoding), and transformation (aggregation, feature engineering).\n\n- **Tools & Automation:** Use of reliable preprocessing tools (e.g., *Pandas*, *NumPy*, *Scikit-learn*, *Trifacta*, *Talend*) and automated pipelines within ML frameworks (e.g., *TensorFlow*, *PyTorch*) to ensure repeatability and minimize human error.\n\n- **Quality Assurance:** Validation of preprocessed data through statistical checks, automated quality reports, and preprocessing logs documenting actions, rationales, and outcomes.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Clear assignment of roles (data stewards, engineers) responsible for preprocessing, with documented approval processes and audit trails.\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Reports showing how preprocessing enhances model performance and mitigates bias, error, or drift.\n\n- **Compliance & Improvement:** Evidence that preprocessing adheres to regulatory standards (e.g., GDPR, ISO/IEC 27001) and is periodically reviewed for optimization.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **standardized, auditable, and\nwell-governed preprocessing workflows** that ensure clean, consistent,\nand reliable AI inputs.",
  "DI-7": "To comply with **DI-7 (\"Are third-party data sources evaluated for\nquality and reliability?\")**, organizations must show they\n**systematically assess, validate, and monitor external data providers**\nto ensure the accuracy, integrity, and ethical use of third-party data.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Third-Party Data Policy:** Formal documentation defining evaluation criteria such as data accuracy, completeness, timeliness, and provider reliability.\n\n- **Vendor Due Diligence:** Records of quality and compliance assessments of data providers, including audits of data collection methods, certification checks (e.g., ISO, GDPR), and risk evaluations.\n\n- **Contracts & SLAs:** Agreements outlining data quality standards, update frequencies, performance metrics, and responsibilities for correction and security.\n\n- **Validation & Monitoring:** Regular testing of third-party data against internal benchmarks, anomaly detection processes, and dashboards tracking ongoing data quality.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Defined approval frameworks for onboarding providers, assigning accountability to procurement or data governance teams.\n\n- **Audit & Issue Management:** Logs of periodic reviews, incidents, and corrective actions tied to provider performance.\n\n- **Compliance & Ethics:** Documentation confirming adherence to legal frameworks (e.g., GDPR, Australian Privacy Act) and ethical sourcing standards.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **formalized, auditable process for\nvetting, monitoring, and governing third-party data** to protect AI\nintegrity and stakeholder trust.",
  "DI-8": "To comply with **DI-8 (\"How do you track data lineage to ensure\ntraceability across the AI lifecycle?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate that **all data flowsfrom source to AI outputare\ntraceable, documented, and auditable** to ensure transparency,\naccountability, and compliance.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Data Lineage Policy:** Formal policy defining the scope, processes, and responsibilities for capturing and maintaining end-to-end lineage across all AI lifecycle stages.\n\n- **Tools & Automation:** Use of lineage tracking systems (e.g., *Collibra*, *Alation*, *Informatica*, *Apache Atlas*, *MLflow*) to automatically record metadata about data origins, transformations, and destinations.\n\n- **Documentation & Visualization:** Lineage diagrams and metadata logs showing how data is collected, processed, and used by specific models or outputs.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Appointed data stewards ensure lineage accuracy and approve any workflow or source changes impacting traceability.\n\n- **Audit & Monitoring:** Continuous monitoring dashboards, periodic lineage validation, and internal/external audits confirming completeness and integrity.\n\n- **Regulatory Compliance:** Alignment with GDPR accountability principles, ISO/IEC 27001, and ethical AI transparency requirements.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Regular updates to lineage tools, processes, and policies based on feedback and lessons learned.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **automated, verifiable lineage\ntracking** that enables complete traceability of data through the entire\nAI lifecycle.",
  "RE-1": "To comply with **RE-1 (\"How do you test the AI system to ensure\nconsistent performance under different conditions?\")**, organizations\nmust show that **systematic, repeatable testing is performed across\nvaried scenarios** to verify stability, accuracy, and robustness.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Testing Policies & Frameworks:** A documented *AI Testing Policy* defining performance benchmarks, stress testing standards, and test coverage (normal, edge, and failure conditions).\n\n- **Test Plans & Cases:** Comprehensive test documentation, including automated test scripts, scenario matrices, and success criteria.\n\n- **Tools & Technologies:** Use of model evaluation and performance tools (*MLflow*, *TensorFlow Model Analysis*, *JMeter*, *Locust*) and simulated environments to test real-world variability.\n\n- **Performance Metrics:** Reports tracking accuracy, latency, recall, and system resource usage during tests.\n\n- **Stress & Edge Testing:** Evidence of high-load or boundary-condition testing to expose weaknesses.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Clear ownership (QA engineers, validators) with sign-offs, reviews, and issue tracking logs.\n\n- **Continuous Testing:** Regression testing after updates, version control logs, and ongoing monitoring for drift.\n\n- **Compliance:** Alignment with ISO/IEC, NIST, and industry-specific reliability standards.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **structured, evidence-based\nreliability testing** that validates consistent AI performance under\ndiverse operational conditions and data variations.",
  "RE-2": "To comply with **RE-2 (\"Have you conducted stress tests or simulations\nto evaluate the system's robustness?\")**, organizations must prove that\ntheir AI systems are **systematically tested under extreme and adverse\nconditions** to identify weaknesses and confirm resilience.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Stress Testing Policy:** A documented framework defining test objectives, scenarios (e.g., high data loads, hardware failures), and success criteria for robustness.\n\n- **Test Plans & Simulations:** Detailed plans and scenarios replicating extreme operational conditions, such as data spikes, network outages, or adversarial inputs.\n\n- **Execution Evidence:** Test logs, performance metrics (e.g., latency, throughput), and simulation results showing system behaviour under stress.\n\n- **Tools & Platforms:** Use of testing tools like *Apache JMeter*, *Locust*, *Chaos Monkey*, or *Gremlin*, and monitoring systems to track performance degradation.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Clear roles, stakeholder reviews, and sign-offs validating test results.\n\n- **Resilience & Mitigation:** Records of system improvements (e.g., redundancy, autoscaling, failover mechanisms) derived from stress test findings.\n\n- **Continuous Testing:** Evidence of periodic or event-triggered stress tests and ongoing monitoring for degradation.\n\n- **Compliance:** Alignment with NIST, ISO 27001, and ethical testing standards.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **formalized, repeatable stress\ntesting and simulation processes** that validate the AI system's ability\nto remain stable and resilient under pressure.",
  "RE-3": "To comply with **RE-3 (\"What mechanisms are in place to monitor the\nsystem for issues like model drift or degradation?\")**, organizations\nmust demonstrate **continuous, automated monitoring** to detect and\naddress performance decay or data drift in AI systems.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Monitoring Policy:** Formal documentation defining *model drift*, detection thresholds, and response protocols for degradation events.\n\n- **Monitoring Tools:** Use of automated platforms like *Evidently AI*, *Fiddler*, *Databricks ML Monitoring*, or in-house dashboards tracking KPIs such as accuracy, recall, and latency.\n\n- **Baseline Metrics & Thresholds:** Established reference performance levels with alert triggers for deviations beyond acceptable limits.\n\n- **Drift Detection Processes:** Regular data and concept drift analyses, supported by statistical tests or retraining triggers when significant variance is detected.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Assigned monitoring roles (data scientists, ML engineers), review boards, and approval workflows for corrective actions.\n\n- **Incident Management:** Logs of detected drift events, root-cause analysis, retraining records, and fallback mechanisms (e.g., backup models).\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Documentation of lessons learned, tool enhancements, and updated policies based on past incidents.\n\n- **Regulatory Compliance:** Alignment with ISO/IEC, NIST, and GDPR accountability standards.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **automated, policy-driven AI\nperformance monitoring** with clear thresholds, alerts, and governance\nto prevent unnoticed degradation.",
  "RE-4": "To comply with **RE-4 (\"How do you define success metrics for the\nreliability of your AI system?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that\n**reliability metrics are clearly defined, measurable, aligned with\nobjectives, and regularly monitored**.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Metric Definition Policy:** A documented process for identifying, approving, and maintaining reliability metrics aligned with organizational goals, ethics, and regulatory obligations.\n\n- **Reliability Metrics Framework:** A standardized method ensuring consistent, comparable definitions of success across systems and projects.\n\n- **Defined Metrics:** Clear KPIs such as accuracy, precision, recall, uptime, latency, error rate, fault tolerance, and drift thresholds.\n\n- **Benchmarking:** Baseline performance metrics and comparisons against industry standards or historical performance.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Assigned roles for defining, reviewing, and validating metrics, with formal approval and periodic review cycles.\n\n- **Monitoring Tools:** Dashboards and observability platforms (e.g., *Datadog*, *MLflow*, *Power BI*) used to track reliability metrics in real-time.\n\n- **Alerts & Incident Management:** Threshold triggers, deviation alerts, and documented remediation actions.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Evidence of metric refinement, lessons learned, and policy updates based on evolving needs or feedback.\n\n- **Compliance:** Demonstrated alignment with ISO/IEC, NIST, and ethical reliability standards.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **structured, transparent, and\ncontinuously reviewed success metrics** to ensure consistent AI\nreliability and accountability.",
  "RE-5": "To comply with **RE-5 (\"Have you identified potential failure points in\nthe system?\")**, organizations must prove they **proactively identify,\ndocument, and mitigate weaknesses** that could compromise AI reliability\nor safety.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Failure Point Identification Policy:** Formal procedures describing how potential failure points are identified, prioritized, and reviewed.\n\n- **Risk Assessments:** Comprehensive system risk analyses (e.g., *Failure Mode and Effects Analysis -- FMEA*) covering data pipelines, model logic, dependencies, infrastructure, and interfaces.\n\n- **Testing & Simulation:** Evidence of stress, edge-case, and fault-injection testing revealing vulnerabilities under adverse conditions.\n\n- **Failure Point Register:** A maintained inventory listing each failure point, its cause, likelihood, impact, and mitigation measures.\n\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Use of observability platforms (e.g., *Datadog*, *New Relic*) to detect anomalies, supported by incident and root-cause logs.\n\n- **Mitigation & Resilience Strategies:** Documented safeguards such as redundancy, failover, auto-scaling, or manual intervention workflows.\n\n- **Governance & Accountability:** Assigned roles for risk management, regular oversight reviews, and post-mortem reports.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned and framework updates based on emerging risks or past incidents.\n\n- **Regulatory Alignment:** Conformance with ISO/IEC, NIST, and ethical reliability standards.\n\n**In short:** Compliance means **systematically mapping and mitigating\npotential AI failure points** through structured risk analysis,\nmonitoring, and continual improvement.",
  "RE-6": "To comply with **RE-6 (\"How do you ensure the system performs\nconsistently under varying conditions (e.g., edge cases)?\")**,\norganizations must prove they **anticipate, test, and monitor AI\nperformance** across both normal and extreme operating conditions.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Consistency & Edge Case Policy:** A documented approach defining edge cases, outlining testing strategies, and integrating them into the organization's AI risk framework.\n\n- **Testing Frameworks:** Comprehensive test plans covering diverse and adversarial scenarios (e.g., noisy inputs, high-load environments, or unusual data distributions).\n\n- **Simulation Environments:** Use of virtual testbeds and synthetic datasets to replicate rare or extreme events (e.g., *AnyLogic*, *SimPy*, *CleverHans*, *ART*).\n\n- **Performance Validation:** Logs and reports demonstrating consistent accuracy, latency, and reliability under tested variations.\n\n- **Monitoring & Analytics Tools:** Real-time observability systems detecting inconsistencies and triggering alerts during live operation.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Designated testing leads, stakeholder reviews, and approval workflows for validation outcomes.\n\n- **Incident & Recovery Plans:** Documented mitigation strategies for failures caused by edge conditions.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned, periodic framework updates, and benchmarking against industry standards.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **structured edge case identification,\nrigorous testing, and proactive monitoring** to ensure AI reliability\nacross all possible operating conditions.",
  "RE-7": "To comply with **RE-7 (\"Are there mechanisms for detecting and\naddressing system downtime or errors?\")**, organizations must show they\nhave **real-time detection, alerting, and rapid recovery processes** to\nmaintain AI system reliability and availability.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Incident Detection & Response Policies:** Formal procedures defining how downtime or errors are identified, escalated, and resolved, including defined alert thresholds.\n\n- **Monitoring Tools:** Use of APM and observability platforms (e.g., *Datadog*, *New Relic*, *Prometheus*, *WhyLabs*) to track uptime, performance, and error rates in real time.\n\n- **Alerting Systems:** Automated alerts for anomalies, outages, or degradation events.\n\n- **Error Logs & Downtime Reports:** Centralized log management (e.g., *Splunk*, *ELK Stack*) with documented root-cause analyses and corrective actions.\n\n- **Redundancy & Failover:** Tested failover systems, backup servers, and load-balancing configurations to reduce single points of failure.\n\n- **Incident Management:** Response logs, post-incident reviews, and lessons-learned documentation.\n\n- **Preventive Maintenance:** Evidence of patching, monitoring, and proactive risk mitigation activities.\n\n- **SLAs & Compliance:** Defined uptime targets, regulatory alignment (ISO/IEC 27001), and accountability through governance review.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Ongoing updates to tools and response frameworks based on incident history.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **structured monitoring, automated\ndetection, and rapid response mechanisms** to minimize downtime and\nmaintain AI system resilience.",
  "RE-8": "To comply with **RE-8 (\"How often is the AI model tested and retrained\nto maintain reliability?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that they\n**regularly test, retrain, and document model updates** to ensure\nsustained accuracy and relevance over time.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Model Maintenance Policy:** Formal documentation outlining retraining frequency, performance thresholds, drift detection triggers, and lifecycle reliability requirements.\n\n- **Testing & Retraining Framework:** Defined methodologies for testing, validation, and retraining using approved tools and workflows.\n\n- **Retraining Schedule:** A documented plan specifying regular testing intervals (e.g., monthly/quarterly) and event-driven retraining triggers (e.g., performance drops, data drift).\n\n- **Performance Monitoring:** Continuous tracking of model metrics (accuracy, F1-score, latency) with alerts for deviations.\n\n- **Retraining Records:** Logs of retraining events, version histories, dataset updates, and post-retraining test outcomes.\n\n- **Governance Oversight:** Evidence of review and approval of retraining schedules and validation results.\n\n- **Tools:** Automated platforms for retraining and monitoring (e.g., *MLflow*, *Kubeflow*, *Neptune.ai*).\n\n- **Risk & Compliance:** Risk assessments, fallback mechanisms, and adherence to ISO/NIST standards.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned, policy refinements, and stakeholder feedback integration.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **structured, documented, and\nautomated retraining process** ensuring AI models remain accurate,\nstable, and aligned with changing data and operational environments.",
  "SE-1": "To comply with **SE-1 (\"What measures are in place to protect your AI\nmodels and data from unauthorized access or tampering?\")**,\norganizations must prove they have **comprehensive technical,\nprocedural, and governance controls** safeguarding AI assets.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **AI Security & Data Protection Policies:** Formal documentation covering encryption, access control, and incident response for AI models and datasets.\n\n- **Access Controls:** Role-based access control (RBAC), principle of least privilege (PoLP), MFA, and detailed authorization logs.\n\n- **Encryption Standards:** AES-256 for data at rest, TLS 1.2+ for data in transit, and secure key management via HSM or cloud key vaults.\n\n- **Network & Infrastructure Security:** Firewalls, IDS/IPS, zero-trust architecture, and hardened containerized deployments (e.g., Docker/Kubernetes).\n\n- **Model Integrity Controls:** Model hashing, tamper-evident repositories, versioning, and runtime integrity monitoring.\n\n- **Monitoring & Alerts:** Centralized logging (e.g., Splunk, LogRhythm) and anomaly detection for suspicious access or data manipulation.\n\n- **Regular Assessments:** Vulnerability scanning, penetration testing, and third-party audits of AI systems.\n\n- **Incident Response & Recovery:** Defined procedures for containment, remediation, and secure backup recovery.\n\n- **Compliance & Training:** Alignment with ISO 27001/SOC 2 and ongoing staff security awareness.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **layered security, continuous\nmonitoring, and proactive governance** to prevent unauthorized access or\ntampering with AI models and data.",
  "SE-2": "To comply with **SE-2 (\"How do you handle adversarial risks, such as\nattacks designed to manipulate the AI's outputs?\")**, organizations must\nshow they have **policies, testing, and mitigation mechanisms** to\nprevent and respond to adversarial manipulation of AI models and data.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Adversarial Risk Policy:** Formal documentation defining adversarial threats (e.g., data poisoning, evasion), assessment methods, and response plans.\n\n- **Threat Modelling:** Reports analyzing vulnerabilities and likely attack vectors, supported by simulated adversarial attack tests.\n\n- **Model Hardening:** Defensive measures such as adversarial training, input validation, gradient masking, and regularization to reduce susceptibility.\n\n- **Monitoring & Detection:** Automated anomaly detection systems to flag sudden performance drops, unusual input patterns, or tampering attempts.\n\n- **Access Controls:** RBAC, API authentication, and rate limiting to restrict potential attack surfaces.\n\n- **Data Protection:** Input filtering, dataset audits, and encryption of model parameters to prevent poisoning or manipulation.\n\n- **Incident Response:** Dedicated adversarial incident response plans with defined containment, forensics, and retraining steps.\n\n- **Compliance & Governance:** Alignment with standards (e.g., NIST Adversarial ML RMF, ISO/IEC AI Security) and regular oversight reviews.\n\n- **Training & Awareness:** Staff education on identifying and mitigating adversarial risks.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proactive threat modelling, robust\ndefensive design, and ongoing monitoring** to protect AI systems from\nadversarial manipulation.",
  "SE-3": "To comply with **SE-3 (\"Do you conduct regular security audits or\npenetration testing on your AI systems?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate a **structured, repeatable, and well-documented process**\nfor assessing AI security vulnerabilities and remediating findings.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Security Audit & Penetration Testing Policies:** Formal policies defining testing frequency, scope (AI models, APIs, infrastructure), and procedures for addressing findings.\n\n- **Audit & Testing Records:** Reports documenting vulnerabilities discovered, remediation actions taken, and adherence to standards (e.g., ISO/IEC 27001, NIST AI RMF).\n\n- **Scope & Methodology:** Defined scope covering models, data pipelines, and IT systems, using frameworks such as *OWASP Top 10 for AI* or *MITRE ATT&CK for AI*.\n\n- **Tools & Technology:** Use of automated scanners and specialized AI testing tools (e.g., Adversarial Robustness Toolbox, Burp Suite, Nessus).\n\n- **Governance Oversight:** Roles assigned for conducting, reviewing, and validating audit outcomes; oversight committee reviews and approvals.\n\n- **Incident & Risk Management:** Documentation of remediation logs, incident reports, and post-test reviews.\n\n- **Third-Party Assessments:** Independent audits or penetration tests conducted by external AI security specialists.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned and updates to testing procedures based on evolving threats.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **regular AI-focused audits and\npenetration tests** with verified follow-up, governance oversight, and\ncontinuous vulnerability management.",
  "SE-4": "To comply with **SE-4 (\"How do you secure the AI model during training,\ndeployment, and operation?\")**, organizations must show evidence of\n**end-to-end security controls** that protect the AI model and its data\nacross all lifecycle stages  training, deployment, and operation.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** AI security and data protection policies defining controls for each lifecycle phase, with assigned roles and responsibilities.\n\n- **Secure Training Practices:** Use of isolated training environments (e.g., Docker, Kubernetes), RBAC, activity logging, and secure preprocessing or anonymization of training data.\n\n- **Secure Deployment:** Hardened environments, version control (e.g., DVC, GitHub Enterprise), encrypted APIs with OAuth and TLS, and network segmentation via firewalls or IDS/IPS.\n\n- **Operational Security:** Real-time monitoring, tamper detection (e.g., cryptographic hashing), and comprehensive access and change logs.\n\n- **Access Control:** RBAC, MFA, and periodic privileged-access reviews to limit exposure.\n\n- **Integrity & Resilience:** Model integrity verification, encrypted backups, and tested recovery processes.\n\n- **Vulnerability & Patch Management:** Regular vulnerability scans, dependency updates, and patch tracking.\n\n- **Governance & Incident Response:** Oversight committee reviews, incident response plans, and post-incident improvement records.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demands **secure-by-design AI lifecycle\nmanagement**  protecting the model, data, and environments through\nencryption, isolation, monitoring, and continuous governance.",
  "SE-5": "To comply with **SE-5 (\"Are there safeguards to prevent unauthorized\nmodifications to the AI model?\")**, organizations must demonstrate\n**strong access control, integrity verification, and change management**\nmechanisms that protect AI models from tampering or unapproved\nalterations.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** AI model security and change management policies defining approval workflows, monitoring, and incident response for unauthorized modifications.\n\n- **Access Controls:** Role-based access control (RBAC), least-privilege permissions, MFA, and secure API authentication to restrict model interaction.\n\n- **Logging & Monitoring:** Continuous logging of all access and modification attempts, supported by anomaly detection and automated alerts for suspicious activity.\n\n- **Integrity Verification:** Cryptographic hashing, checksums, and tamper-evident mechanisms (e.g., blockchain tracking) to verify model authenticity.\n\n- **Secure Development & Deployment:** Use of secure repositories (e.g., GitHub Enterprise) with commit approvals, branch protections, and CI/CD pipelines that include automated security checks.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Required approvals before model changes, routine audits of modification records, and governance committee reviews.\n\n- **Incident Response:** Plans detailing detection, containment, and recovery procedures following unauthorized changes.\n\n- **Compliance & Training:** Alignment with NIST AI RMF, ISO/IEC 27001, and ongoing staff education on secure model management.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **robust technical and procedural\nsafeguards** ensuring only authorized, traceable, and verified\nmodifications to AI models.",
  "SE-6": "To comply with **SE-6 (\"What measures are in place to protect the system\nagainst adversarial attacks such as input manipulation?\")**,\norganizations must demonstrate **comprehensive defences** against\nadversarial threats that aim to deceive or manipulate AI models.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Adversarial risk mitigation and AI security policies detailing how input manipulation and related threats are identified, tested, and managed.\n\n- **Threat Modeling & Risk Assessment:** Documentation identifying adversarial risks (e.g., data poisoning, evasion, inversion) and their potential impact.\n\n- **Defensive Techniques:** Evidence of adversarial training, robust input validation, regularization, and noise resistance techniques to strengthen model resilience.\n\n- **Monitoring & Detection:** Use of anomaly detection, behavioural analytics, and alert systems to identify suspicious inputs or outputs.\n\n- **Secure Deployment:** Enforced API security (rate limiting, authentication, input validation) and model version control to prevent exploitation.\n\n- **Tools & Frameworks:** Use of testing tools like *Adversarial Robustness Toolbox* or *CleverHans*, and AI security platforms such as *CalypsoAI* or *Robust Intelligence*.\n\n- **Incident Response:** Plans and logs documenting adversarial attack handling, root cause analysis, and recovery measures.\n\n- **Governance & Training:** Defined responsibilities, regular audits, and staff education on adversarial risks.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proactive adversarial risk\nmanagement** through testing, monitoring, policy control, and continuous\nresilience improvement.",
  "SE-7": "To comply with **SE-7 (\"How do you protect sensitive data in your AI\npipelines?\")**, organizations must show evidence of **robust data\nprotection mechanisms**from collection to disposalcovering\nencryption, tokenization, access control, and governance.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Data protection and AI pipeline security policies specifying encryption, tokenization, anonymization, and roles responsible for data safeguarding.\n\n- **Encryption Practices:** Use of AES-256 encryption for data at rest and TLS 1.2+ for data in transit, plus secure key management (e.g., AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault) with rotation and RBAC access.\n\n- **Tokenization & Anonymization:** Implementation of tokenization and privacy-enhancing techniques such as differential privacy, hashing, or suppression of identifiers.\n\n- **Access Controls:** RBAC enforcement and least-privilege access for data handling across all pipeline stages.\n\n- **Secure Pipeline Design:** Segmentation of sensitive data, secure VPC or container environments, and continuous monitoring for anomalies.\n\n- **Data Masking & Synthetic Data:** Dynamic masking during development and synthetic data use to prevent unnecessary exposure.\n\n- **Compliance & Governance:** Alignment with GDPR, Australian Privacy Act 1988, and ISO 27001, supported by governance reviews and data classification frameworks.\n\n- **Incident Response:** Data-breach plans and post-incident improvement records.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **end-to-end data protection**\nensuring encryption, minimization, and controlled handling of sensitive\ndata throughout the AI lifecycle.",
  "SE-8": "To comply with **SE-8 (\"Are there regular vulnerability assessments and\npenetration tests performed on the AI system?\")**, organizations must\nshow that they **routinely identify, test, and remediate** security\nweaknesses across all AI components.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Documented vulnerability management and penetration testing policies defining frequency, scope, methodologies (e.g., OWASP AI, MITRE ATLAS), and remediation workflows.\n\n- **Testing Records:** Completed vulnerability and penetration test reports outlining findings, severity, remediation actions, and resolution timelines.\n\n- **Scope & Methodology:** Evidence that testing covers AI models, datasets, APIs, infrastructure, and deployment pipelines using static and dynamic analysis.\n\n- **Tools & Frameworks:** Use of scanning tools (e.g., Nessus, Qualys, OpenVAS) and AI-specific tools (e.g., Adversarial Robustness Toolbox, CleverHans, Burp Suite, Metasploit).\n\n- **Governance:** Scheduled testing cadence, assigned responsibilities, and audit logs ensuring tests occur consistently and findings are tracked.\n\n- **Remediation & Risk Management:** Logs showing timely resolution and inclusion of vulnerabilities in broader risk registers.\n\n- **Third-Party Validation:** Independent external penetration tests and certifications (e.g., ISO 27001, SOC 2) verifying internal practices.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned, updated methodologies, and training on emerging AI attack vectors.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **structured, recurring security\ntesting** with proven remediation, oversight, and independent validation\nof AI system resilience.",
  "PR-1": "To comply with **PR-1 (\"How do you ensure compliance with data privacy\nlaws such as GDPR or the Australian Privacy Act?\")**, organizations must\nshow **end-to-end data privacy governance** that aligns with legal,\ntechnical, and procedural obligations.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Published Privacy Policy, Data Protection Policy, and Consent Management Policy outlining how personal data is collected, processed, stored, and deleted.\n\n- **Data Mapping:** Comprehensive data inventories and Records of Processing Activities (RoPA) identifying data flows, storage, and retention timelines.\n\n- **Data Subject Rights:** Processes and logs demonstrating timely handling of requests for access, correction, erasure, and portability.\n\n- **Legal Compliance:** Evidence of Privacy Impact Assessments (PIAs), regulator alignment (GDPR, Australian Privacy Act), and vendor compliance documentation.\n\n- **Consent Management:** Records and systems tracking user consent and withdrawal; cookie consent tools.\n\n- **Protection Measures:** RBAC enforcement, encryption, anonymization, pseudonymization, and data minimization.\n\n- **Governance:** Appointment of a Data Protection Officer (DPO) and active privacy oversight committees.\n\n- **Monitoring & Auditing:** Regular audits, breach logs, and continuous monitoring for compliance issues.\n\n- **Certifications:** ISO/IEC 27701, GDPR certifications, and third-party audit reports.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demands **proactive privacy governance,\ndemonstrable consent and data-protection practices, and continuous\nalignment with evolving regulatory requirements.**",
  "PR-2": "To comply with **PR-2 (\"What techniques do you use to protect personal\ndata in your AI workflows?\")**, organizations must demonstrate the use\nof **anonymization, pseudonymization, and other privacy-enhancing\ntechniques** that prevent re-identification while maintaining data\nutility.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Formal Data Protection and Privacy Policies defining anonymization and pseudonymization processes and when each method applies across the AI lifecycle.\n\n- **Techniques & Implementation:** Evidence of anonymization (generalization, suppression, differential privacy) and pseudonymization (tokenization, encryption, unique identifiers) applied during data preprocessing.\n\n- **Data Management:** Data flow maps and processing records showing where and how personal identifiers are transformed or removed.\n\n- **Tools & Technologies:** Use of PETs (e.g., OpenDP, Privitar, Protegrity), k-anonymity algorithms, and secure key management via AWS KMS or Azure Key Vault.\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Regular re-identification risk assessments and vulnerability tests to ensure transformations are irreversible and effective.\n\n- **Access Control & Segmentation:** RBAC enforcement, separation of raw and anonymized datasets, and encrypted key management.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** DPO supervision, internal audits, and periodic policy updates reflecting evolving best practices.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **provable de-identification\nprocesses, tested privacy techniques, and continuous governance to\nprevent re-identification risks** in AI workflows.",
  "PR-3": "To comply with **PR-3 (\"How do you manage user consent for data\ncollection and processing?\")**, organizations must show that they\n**obtain, record, and manage consent transparently** in line with\nprivacy laws like GDPR and the Australian Privacy Act.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Documented Consent Management Policy and Privacy Policy detailing how consent is requested, stored, and withdrawn.\n\n- **Consent Mechanisms:** Clear, jargon-free consent forms; granular opt-in options; pop-ups or cookie banners compliant with privacy regulations.\n\n- **Records & Audit Trails:** Logs capturing when and how consent was obtained, what users agreed to, and the tools used (e.g., OneTrust, TrustArc, Cookiebot).\n\n- **Withdrawal & Updates:** Easy interfaces for users to revoke consent; records showing timely updates to data practices and renewed consent requests.\n\n- **Transparency & Communication:** Layered privacy notices and accessible statements clarifying purposes of data use.\n\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Systems ensuring data is not processed without valid consent, with regular reviews and compliance dashboards.\n\n- **Governance & Training:** Staff training on consent management and incident response plans for consent breaches.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **clear, trackable, and\nuser-controlled consent processes** supported by policies, automation\ntools, and continuous oversight.",
  "PR-4": "To comply with **PR-4 (\"How do you ensure that personal data is not used\nbeyond its original purpose?\")**, organizations must demonstrate strict\n**purpose limitation controls** that prevent personal data from being\naccessed or repurposed outside its intended scope.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Data Usage Policy defining authorized purposes and prohibiting secondary use; Privacy Policy publicly stating data purposes.\n\n- **Consent & Documentation:** Consent logs confirming data use aligns with user agreements and that consent specifies exact purposes.\n\n- **Data Governance:** Data classification and tagging systems labeling data with permitted uses; lifecycle documentation tracking data from collection to disposal.\n\n- **Access Controls:** RBAC restricting access to data only for personnel and applications with a legitimate, consented purpose.\n\n- **Monitoring & Auditing:** Automated tools and alerts detecting unauthorized access or usage beyond stated purposes; regular audits of data flows.\n\n- **Minimization & Retention:** Collecting only essential data and securely deleting or anonymizing it once its original purpose is fulfilled.\n\n- **Training & Oversight:** Staff awareness programs, governance committees, and PIAs verifying ongoing compliance.\n\n**In short:** Compliance relies on **clear purpose definition, strict\naccess and monitoring controls, and transparent lifecycle management**\nensuring data is used solely for its intended purpose.",
  "PR-5": "To comply with **PR-5 (\"What methods do you use to anonymize or\npseudonymize personal data?\")**, organizations must demonstrate\n**technical and procedural safeguards** ensuring sensitive data cannot\nbe traced back to individuals while maintaining analytical utility for\nAI processing.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Data Protection and Privacy Policies defining anonymization/pseudonymization methods, responsible roles, and governance structures.\n\n- **Anonymization Techniques:** Use of **generalization**, **suppression**, **differential privacy**, or **data aggregation** to irreversibly remove identifiers; re-identification risk assessments proving effectiveness.\n\n- **Pseudonymization Techniques:** Use of **tokenization**, **hashing (with salt)**, and **encryption (e.g., AES-256)** to replace identifiers with non-identifiable values, supported by secure key management (AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault, HashiCorp Vault).\n\n- **Implementation in Workflows:** Application of de-identification across all data lifecycle stagescollection, preprocessing, storage, and archivalpreferably automated to reduce human error.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** DPO review and audit logs confirming anonymization steps and approvals.\n\n- **Regulatory Compliance:** Evidence aligning with **GDPR Article 25**, **Australian Privacy Act**, and **ISO/IEC 27701** standards.\n\n- **Monitoring & Testing:** Regular re-identification testing and continuous policy updates based on new threats or technologies.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proven, well-governed anonymization\nand pseudonymization methods** applied consistently, monitored\ncontinuously, and validated against re-identification risks.",
  "PR-6": "To comply with **PR-6 (\"How do you monitor and prevent privacy\nviolations during system operation?\")**, organizations must show that\nthey **actively detect, prevent, and respond** to privacy breaches\nthrough continuous monitoring and governance mechanisms.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Privacy Monitoring and Incident Management Policies outlining monitoring, detection, and escalation protocols for privacy breaches.\n\n- **Monitoring Tools & Technology:** Implementation of **Data Loss Prevention (DLP)**, **AI monitoring**, and **logging tools** (e.g., access logs, transaction logs) that track and alert on suspicious activity or unauthorized access.\n\n- **Access Control:** RBAC and access segmentation ensuring only authorized personnel can access sensitive data, with regular access reviews.\n\n- **Incident Response & Mitigation:** Formal response plan defining containment, notification, and remediation processes; breach logs documenting actions taken.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Active involvement of a **Privacy Governance Committee** or **Data Protection Officer (DPO)** reviewing reports and ensuring accountability.\n\n- **Testing & Audits:** Regular validation of monitoring systems and penetration tests to confirm detection capability.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned reports, feedback loops, and system updates based on incident reviews or regulatory changes.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proactive monitoring, strict access\ncontrol, automated alerts, and a clear response process** to prevent and\nmitigate operational privacy violations.",
  "PR-7": "To comply with **PR-7 (\"Are privacy risks evaluated during both the\ndevelopment and deployment phases?\")**, organizations must show a\n**lifecycle-based approach** to identifying and mitigating privacy risks\nfrom design to real-world operation.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A **Privacy by Design Policy** integrating privacy into every stage of the AI lifecycle; documented risk assessment procedures for both development and deployment.\n\n- **Privacy Risk Assessments:** **Privacy Impact Assessments (PIAs)** and, where applicable, **Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs)** evaluating data flows, design decisions, and operational risks.\n\n- **Design & Development Practices:** Evidence of privacy-focused design artifacts (e.g., data flow diagrams, threat models) and adherence to secure coding standards emphasizing encryption, access controls, and data minimization.\n\n- **Deployment Safeguards:** Testing and monitoring in live environments to detect data leakage or unauthorized access, with mitigation plans in place.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Reviews and approvals from governance committees or the **Data Protection Officer (DPO)**, with audit trails documenting identified risks and resolutions.\n\n- **Training & Continuous Improvement:** Developer and operator training on privacy risk mitigation; lessons-learned reviews feeding into process updates.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proactive, documented, and ongoing\nprivacy risk evaluation** throughout AI system development and\ndeployment.",
  "PR-8": "To comply with **PR-8 (\"What steps are taken to ensure compliance with\ncross-border data protection laws?\")**, organizations must demonstrate\nthat all **international data transfers** follow lawful mechanisms and\nmaintain equivalent privacy safeguards across jurisdictions.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A **Data Transfer Policy** defining legal bases, safeguards, and jurisdictions for data movement; updated **Privacy Policy** informing users about cross-border transfers.\n\n- **Legal Agreements:** Use of **Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs)**, **Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs)**, and **Data Processing Agreements (DPAs)** ensuring partner and vendor compliance.\n\n- **Risk Assessments:** Completion of **Transfer Impact Assessments (TIAs)** and **Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs)** evaluating destination-country risks and documenting mitigations.\n\n- **Technical Safeguards:** Encryption, anonymization, and pseudonymization applied to all data in transit and at rest across borders.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** DPO review and, where applicable, a **Cross-Border Data Governance Committee** approving or monitoring transfers.\n\n- **Compliance Monitoring:** Tools auditing data flows and maintaining records of regulatory submissions or adequacy decisions.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Regular policy reviews following regulatory changes, audits, or incidents.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **clear legal frameworks, technical\nsafeguards, documented risk assessments, and active oversight** to\nprotect data across multiple jurisdictions.",
  "SA-1": "To comply with **SA-1 (\"Have you conducted a safety impact assessment\nfor the AI system?\")**, organizations must prove they **formally assess,\ndocument, and mitigate potential harms** their AI systems could cause to\npeople, operations, or the environment.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A **Safety Assessment Policy** and **Risk Management Policy** mandating Safety Impact Assessments (SIAs) before deployment, with guidance on identifying and addressing safety risks.\n\n- **SIA Reports:** Comprehensive records showing identified risks, likelihood and severity ratings, and implemented mitigations (e.g., fail-safes, redundancy, shutdown mechanisms).\n\n- **Risk Identification & Mitigation:** Use of structured methods (risk matrices, FMEA, HACCP) to prioritize and mitigate potential failures or unsafe behaviours.\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Evidence of pre-deployment and simulation testing to verify safety performance and effectiveness of mitigations.\n\n- **Standards Alignment:** Demonstrated compliance with **ISO/IEC 23894 (AI Safety)**, **ISO 31000**, and **NIST AI RMF**.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Review by a **safety or AI governance committee** and periodic safety audits.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned from incidents and policy updates.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **structured, auditable SIA\nprocess** ensuring AI systems are designed, tested, and monitored to\nminimize harm and meet recognized safety standards.",
  "SA-2": "To comply with **SA-2 (\"How do you ensure that the system does not cause\nharm in unintended ways?\")**, organizations must show a **proactive,\nend-to-end strategy** for identifying, testing, and mitigating\nunintentional harm from AI systems.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** An **AI Safety Policy** and **Risk Management Policy** defining how unintended harm is detected, prevented, and managed throughout the system lifecycle.\n\n- **Risk Assessment:** Documentation of **hazard identification**, **FMEA/FTA analyses**, and **Safety Impact Assessments (SIAs)** covering risks such as bias, misinterpretation, or malfunction.\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Results from **scenario testing**, **edge case analysis**, and **fail-safe validation** demonstrating safe system behaviour under stress or anomalies.\n\n- **System Design Controls:** Inclusion of **human-in-the-loop oversight**, **fail-safe mechanisms**, and **Explainable AI (XAI)** features enabling detection of unintended outcomes.\n\n- **Continuous Monitoring:** Tools and logs tracking anomalies, false positives/negatives, and user feedback for real-time harm prevention.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Safety review committees, incident response frameworks, and periodic audits ensuring accountability.\n\n- **Training & Improvement:** Ongoing staff education and policy updates based on incidents or lessons learned.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **robust policies, risk-based testing,\nmonitoring, and human oversight** to detect and prevent AI from causing\nharm unintentionally.",
  "SA-3": "To comply with **SA-3 (\"Are there monitoring tools in place to detect\nunsafe system behaviour in real-time?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate **active, automated monitoring and response capabilities**\nthat can detect, alert, and mitigate unsafe AI behaviour as it happens.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A **Real-Time Monitoring Policy** and **Incident Response Policy** defining how unsafe behaviour is detected, logged, and escalated.\n\n- **Monitoring Tools & Technology:** Implementation of **anomaly detection**, **real-time logging**, and **alerting platforms** (e.g., Splunk, DataDog, Prometheus) integrated with SIEM or ticketing systems (ServiceNow, Jira).\n\n- **Anomaly Detection & Fail-Safes:** Predefined thresholds, automated shutdowns, and **AI-specific drift detection** tools that identify deviations in inputs, outputs, or behaviour.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Regular audits, oversight committees, and documented root-cause analyses for detected issues.\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Simulation of unsafe scenarios and validation of tool performance and response speed.\n\n- **Training & Metrics:** Trained operators with clear monitoring guidelines; KPIs measuring detection rate and response time.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons-learned reviews and tool enhancements following incidents or near-misses.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **real-time visibility, automated\nanomaly detection, and coordinated incident response** to promptly\ndetect and neutralize unsafe AI behaviour.",
  "SA-4": "To comply with **SA-4 (\"How do you handle situations where the system's\nactions conflict with human safety requirements?\")**, organizations must\nshow that **human safety always overrides system autonomy** through\nrobust policies, fail-safes, and governance.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A **Safety Policy** and **Risk Management Policy** defining escalation paths and decision-making when AI actions conflict with safety rules.\n\n- **Fail-Safe Mechanisms:** Evidence of **automated shutdown protocols**, **emergency stop systems**, and **manual override controls** enabling human intervention at any time.\n\n- **Incident Response Framework:** Plans and logs detailing how safety conflicts are identified, escalated, investigated, and resolved, including post-incident reviews.\n\n- **System Design & Testing:** Human-centric designs, **simulations of conflict scenarios**, and validation reports confirming the AI system prioritizes safety over performance.\n\n- **Monitoring & Detection:** Real-time tools and alerts identifying potential conflicts between AI behaviour and safety requirements.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Safety review boards, audits, and compliance with **ISO/IEC 23894** (AI Safety) and **ISO 31000** (Risk Management).\n\n- **Training & Improvement:** Staff training on override use and incident handling, plus lessons learned driving policy and system updates.\n\n**In short:** Compliance means **humans stay in control**AI must\nstop, alert, or defer whenever safety is at risk.",
  "SA-5": "To comply with **SA-5 (\"Are there emergency protocols for shutting down\nthe system in case of unsafe behaviour?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate **tested, documented, and effective shutdown procedures**\nthat protect users and systems during unsafe events.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A formal **Emergency Shutdown Policy** and **Incident Response Plan** defining when and how shutdowns occur, who authorizes them, and post-shutdown actions.\n\n- **Shutdown Mechanisms:** Presence of **automated shutdown features** (e.g., anomaly-triggered halts) and **manual override controls** allowing human intervention. Redundant systems should support safe degradation or recovery.\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Logs and reports of **simulation and stress testing**, verifying both automatic and manual shutdown effectiveness under various failure scenarios.\n\n- **Monitoring & Detection:** Tools that continuously detect unsafe behaviour and trigger alerts or automated shutdowns when thresholds are breached.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Reviews and audits by safety committees confirming the protocols' reliability and compliance with **ISO/IEC 23894** and **ISO 31000** standards.\n\n- **Training & Documentation:** Operator training records, **emergency playbooks**, and post-incident analyses demonstrating preparedness and learning.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **proven ability to detect danger,\nhalt operations safely, and recover gracefully** through well-defined,\nregularly tested shutdown protocols.",
  "SA-6": "To comply with **SA-6 (\"What steps have you taken to identify and\nmitigate potential risks or harm caused by the AI system?\")**,\norganizations must demonstrate a **structured, proactive risk management\nprocess** that identifies, assesses, and mitigates harms across the AI\nlifecycle.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A formal **Risk Identification and Mitigation Policy** and **Ethical AI Policy** defining responsibilities, review cycles, and harm prevention principles.\n\n- **Risk Assessments:** Documented **risk analyses**, **Safety Impact Assessments (SIAs)**, and **threat modeling** covering bias, unintended outcomes, and system vulnerabilities.\n\n- **Mitigation Strategies:** Implementation of **risk mitigation plans**, algorithm refinements, dataset bias reduction, and **technical safeguards** such as input validation and anomaly detection.\n\n- **Testing & Monitoring:** Simulation, stress testing, and **model validation reports** confirming stability and reliability, supported by **real-time monitoring tools** and incident logs.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Review by **risk oversight committees**, supported by audits ensuring alignment with **ISO/IEC 23894**, **ISO 31000**, and **NIST AI RMF**.\n\n- **Training & Improvement:** Staff training, lessons learned, and regular policy updates to strengthen future risk management.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **end-to-end governance, testing, and\ncontinuous refinement** to minimize potential harm and ensure the AI\nsystem operates safely and ethically.",
  "SA-7": "To comply with **SA-7 (\"Are there fail-safe mechanisms or redundancies\nin place to address unexpected system behaviours?\")**, organizations\nmust prove that **their AI systems can detect, respond to, and recover\nsafely** from unexpected failures or anomalies.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A **Fail-Safe Policy** and **Incident Response Policy** defining activation conditions, responsibilities, and integration with broader safety frameworks.\n\n- **System Design:** Documentation of **fail-safe mechanisms** (e.g., automated shutdowns, rollback to safe states, component isolation) and **redundancy systems** (backup servers, redundant storage, alternate data pathways). Evidence of **graceful degradation** to maintain limited functionality under stress.\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Logs from **simulation, stress, and redundancy tests** confirming mechanisms activate as expected and handoffs occur seamlessly.\n\n- **Monitoring & Detection:** Use of **real-time anomaly detection tools** and **incident logs** showing historical fail-safe activations.\n\n- **Governance & Compliance:** Oversight by safety committees and alignment with **ISO/IEC 23894 (AI Safety)** and **ISO 31000 (Risk Management)**.\n\n- **Training & Playbooks:** Staff trained to execute manual fail-safes, supported by **operational playbooks** for rapid response.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the system is **resilient,\nrecoverable, and capable of protecting people and assets** when failures\noccur unexpectedly.",
  "SA-8": "To comply with **SA-8 (\"Have you conducted safety testing in realistic\nor high-risk scenarios?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that their\nAI systems have been **rigorously tested under real-world and extreme\nconditions** to ensure reliability and safety.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A formal **Safety Testing Policy** defining objectives, methodologies, and reporting; and integration of testing into the overall **Risk Management Framework**.\n\n- **Test Planning & Documentation:** Comprehensive **test plans** and **scenario design documents** showing realistic, edge-case, and high-risk conditionssuch as data anomalies, environmental stressors, and adversarial attacks.\n\n- **Testing & Validation Results:** Detailed **safety test reports**, **simulation results**, and **validation findings** confirming that the AI performs safely under all tested conditions.\n\n- **Monitoring & Incident Management:** Real-time test monitoring logs, **incident records**, and **root cause analyses** from detected issues during testing.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Review and approval by **testing oversight committees** or external auditors, demonstrating adherence to **ISO/IEC 23894 (AI Safety)** and **ISO/IEC 29119 (Software Testing)**.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Documentation of updates, lessons learned, and design adjustments informed by test results.\n\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proving the AI system remains safe\nand stable even in high-risk, unpredictable conditions** through\nstructured, evidence-based safety testing.",
  "IN-1": "To comply with **IN-1 (\"How do you ensure your AI system is accessible\nto a diverse range of users, including those with disabilities or in\nunderserved populations?\")**, organizations must demonstrate a\n**commitment to inclusive, accessible AI design and operation**.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A formal **Accessibility Policy** and **Inclusion & Diversity Policy** referencing standards like **WCAG 2.1**, **ADA**, **Section 508**, or **EN 301 549**, showing organizational commitment to equitable access.\n\n- **User-Centric Design:** Inclusive design documentation with **diverse personas**, accessibility considerations (e.g., screen reader support, high-contrast modes, multilingual capability), and testing with **diverse participants**.\n\n- **Accessibility Features:** Evidence of implemented features such as **speech-to-text, keyboard navigation, voice interaction, and adjustable UI elements**.\n\n- **Testing & Feedback:** Reports from **accessibility testing tools** (e.g., Axe, WAVE, Lighthouse) and stakeholder feedback loops involving advocacy groups and underserved communities.\n\n- **Training & Governance:** Staff training on inclusive design principles and oversight by **inclusion committees** to ensure continuous accessibility improvements.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Regular audits, updates, and accessibility performance tracking.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the AI system is **usable,\ninclusive, and accessible to all individuals**, ensuring equitable\nparticipation regardless of ability, language, or background.",
  "IN-2": "To comply with **IN-2 (\"Were diverse perspectives considered during the\ndesign and development of the system?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate that **a variety of voices, experiences, and disciplines\ninformed the AI system's creation**.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Stakeholder Engagement:** Documentation showing consultations with **diverse end-users**, DEI experts, advocacy groups, and underrepresented communities. Structured feedback mechanisms that ensure insights are incorporated into design decisions.\n\n- **Diverse Team Composition:** Reports showing **inclusive representation** across gender, ethnicity, geography, and roles (technical, ethical, social). Evidence that diverse team members had **decision-making influence**.\n\n- **Inclusive Design Frameworks:** Adoption of principles such as **Microsoft's Inclusive Design** or **Universal Design for Learning (UDL)**, supported by diversity checklists and ethical impact assessments.\n\n- **User Research & Testing:** Proof that user research and testing included diverse participants, influencing usability and accessibility features.\n\n- **Training & Governance:** DEI training for development teams and oversight from **diversity review boards** or inclusion committees.\n\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Documentation showing ongoing refinement based on feedback from marginalized or diverse groups.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that **diversity was embedded from\ndesign to deployment**, ensuring the AI system serves broad, equitable,\nand inclusive user needs.",
  "IN-3": "To comply with **IN-3 (\"Have you audited the system for potential\nexclusionary outcomes or unintended impacts?\")**, organizations must\nshow that **systematic audits are conducted to detect and correct bias\nor harm affecting specific groups**.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A formal **Audit Policy** defining the scope, objectives, and methodology for inclusivity and fairness audits, supported by an **Ethical AI Policy** committing to ongoing impact assessment.\n\n- **Audit Records & Reports:** Completed **audit reports** identifying exclusionary outcomes, demographic disparities, or unintended social/economic impacts, including documented **mitigation plans**.\n\n- **Methodology & Tools:** Use of **established frameworks** (e.g., NIST AI RMF, ISO 27501, IEEE 7000) and bias detection tools like **Fairlearn** or **IBM AI Fairness 360**, alongside fairness metrics (e.g., demographic parity, equalized odds).\n\n- **Data & Stakeholder Engagement:** **Dataset audits**, **disparity analyses**, and **consultation records** showing engagement with affected groups and external experts.\n\n- **Governance & Improvement:** Oversight by **audit committees** or ethics boards, with **corrective actions**, **follow-up audits**, and lessons learned integrated into future updates.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the organization **actively\nidentifies, measures, and mitigates bias or exclusion**, ensuring\nequitable outcomes and continuous fairness improvements in AI\noperations.",
  "IN-4": "To comply with **IN-4 (\"How do you ensure your AI system accounts for\naccessibility requirements (e.g., for users with disabilities)?\")**,\norganizations must demonstrate a **structured and standards-aligned\napproach to accessibility** in design, testing, and maintenance.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Standards:** A documented **Accessibility Policy** referencing **WCAG 2.1**, **ADA**, **EN 301 549**, or equivalent, supported by an **Inclusivity Policy** embedding accessibility in AI lifecycle governance.\n\n- **Accessibility Features:** Evidence of implemented features such as **screen-reader compatibility, speech-to-text/text-to-speech, keyboard navigation, high-contrast modes, scalable text**, and multi-modal inputs (text, voice, visual).\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Reports from **accessibility audits** (using Axe, WAVE, Lighthouse, etc.) and **user testing** with people with disabilities. Demonstrate **feedback loops** where issues were identified and fixed.\n\n- **Design & Training:** Use of **Inclusive or Universal Design frameworks** and training programs for developers to apply accessibility best practices from the outset.\n\n- **Governance & Improvement:** Oversight by **accessibility review committees**, integration with **assistive technologies** (JAWS, NVDA, Braille displays), and **continuous improvement records** documenting updates and lessons learned.\n\n**In short:** Compliance proves the AI system is **usable, equitable,\nand barrier-free**, ensuring full participation for users with diverse\nabilities.",
  "IN-5": "To comply with **IN-5 (\"Are stakeholders from diverse backgrounds\ninvolved in the design and testing of the system?\")**, organizations\nmust show that **diversity and inclusion are actively embedded in\nstakeholder engagement throughout the AI lifecycle**.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Stakeholder Engagement:** Records of consultations, workshops, or interviews with stakeholders from **different genders, ethnicities, ages, and socioeconomic groups**, including marginalized or underserved communities.\n\n- **Diverse Team Composition:** Documentation showing diverse project teams across roles (technical, ethical, and social), supported by **inclusive hiring and participation policies**.\n\n- **Feedback Mechanisms:** Logs capturing stakeholder input, observed inclusivity gaps, and **evidence that feedback informed system refinements**.\n\n- **Testing and Validation:** Reports showing **user testing and beta trials** with demographically diverse participants, emphasizing usability, fairness, and accessibility.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Existence of **diversity review committees** and oversight reports validating stakeholder inclusion across project stages.\n\n- **Training & Frameworks:** Records of DEI training and use of **inclusive design toolkits** or frameworks (e.g., Universal Design, Microsoft Inclusive Design).\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the organization **engaged a\nbroad spectrum of stakeholders** to ensure its AI system is equitable,\nrepresentative, and responsive to the needs of all user groups.",
  "IN-6": "To comply with **IN-6 (\"What steps are taken to ensure that the AI\nsystem serves underrepresented communities effectively?\")**,\norganizations must show a **proactive and measurable commitment to\nequity, representation, and inclusion** in both data and design.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A documented **Inclusivity Policy** and **Diversity and Equity Guidelines** that define how AI systems will address the unique needs of marginalized or underserved groups.\n\n- **Data Representation:** **Data audits** and **diverse dataset sourcing** to ensure demographic and socioeconomic representation, along with bias mitigation or data augmentation where gaps exist.\n\n- **Community Engagement:** Records of **consultations, workshops, and user feedback** involving underrepresented communities to inform design decisions and system refinements.\n\n- **Inclusive Design & Testing:** Design documentation showing **multilingual support, accessibility options, and adaptable interfaces**, tested specifically with users from these communities.\n\n- **Monitoring & Governance:** Ongoing **performance metrics**, **feedback loops**, and oversight by **inclusivity review committees** or independent auditors.\n\n- **Training & Standards Alignment:** Staff training on equity in AI and compliance with standards like **ISO 27501** and **IEEE 7010**.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the AI system is\n**intentionally designed, tested, and governed** to deliver equitable\nbenefits and minimize harm for underrepresented communities.",
  "IN-7": "To comply with **IN-7 (\"Are language, cultural, or regional differences\nconsidered in system design?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that\n**localization and cultural sensitivity are built into every stage** of\nAI system development and deployment.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Guidelines:** A documented **Cultural Sensitivity Policy** and **Localization Guidelines** detailing how language, cultural, and regional factors are incorporated into system design and functionality.\n\n- **Data Representation:** Diverse datasets representing multiple **languages, cultures, and regions**, supported by **data audits** to identify and address representation gaps or biases.\n\n- **Design & Features:** Evidence of **multilingual NLP capabilities**, **localized interfaces**, **region-specific configurations** (dates, currency, regulations), and **culturally adaptive content** that avoids insensitivity or bias.\n\n- **Testing & Validation:** Reports from **localized and bias testing** verifying that the system performs effectively across cultural and regional contexts.\n\n- **Stakeholder Engagement:** Input from **linguists, cultural experts, and regional users** to refine system design and outputs.\n\n- **Governance & Improvement:** Ongoing **monitoring, user feedback loops, and audits** ensuring continuous enhancement of cultural relevance and inclusivity.\n\n**In short:** Compliance proves the AI system is **linguistically\nadaptable, culturally aware, and regionally relevant**, minimizing\nexclusion and maximizing effectiveness across diverse user populations.",
  "IN-8": "To comply with **IN-8 (\"How do you evaluate whether the system is\nunintentionally excluding certain user groups?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate that **structured, ongoing evaluations are conducted to\nidentify and correct exclusionary outcomes**.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Guidelines:** A formal **Inclusivity Assessment Policy** defining how exclusion risks are identified, assessed, and mitigated during the AI lifecycle.\n\n- **Data Representation:** **Dataset audits** ensuring demographic and socioeconomic diversity, with documentation of actions taken to fix underrepresentation or bias.\n\n- **Testing & Evaluation:** Reports from **bias, accessibility, and real-world simulations** assessing how outputs vary across demographic groups using fairness metrics (e.g., disparate impact ratios).\n\n- **User & Community Feedback:** Records of **feedback loops and consultations** with diverse or underrepresented users and advocacy groups to detect usability barriers.\n\n- **Monitoring & Analytics:** **Usage and performance metrics** comparing engagement and outcomes across groups to flag disparities.\n\n- **Tools & Corrective Actions:** Use of **bias detection platforms** (e.g., IBM AI Fairness 360, Fairlearn) and documented remediation efforts with re-evaluations.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Involvement of **inclusivity committees and audits** ensuring regular exclusion assessments.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates the organization **actively\nmonitors, tests, and improves inclusivity**, ensuring no user group is\nunintentionally disadvantaged by the AI system.",
  "SU-1": "To comply with **SU-1 (\"What is the environmental impact of training and\ndeploying your AI system?\")**, organizations must **measure, monitor,\nand minimize** the environmental footprint of their AI lifecycle\nparticularly energy use and carbon emissions.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A clear **Sustainability Policy** outlining goals for reducing AI-related energy consumption and emissions.\n\n- **Impact Assessments:** **Energy usage and carbon footprint reports** (using tools like *CodeCarbon* or *Green Algorithms*), plus **lifecycle assessments** covering development to decommissioning.\n\n- **Metrics & Monitoring:** Benchmark data on energy intensity, computational efficiency, and emission trends, tracked through internal dashboards or cloud provider tools.\n\n- **Optimization Strategies:** Documentation showing **model and infrastructure efficiency improvements**, such as model pruning, workload scheduling, or use of low-power hardware.\n\n- **Renewable Energy & Offsetting:** Evidence of **green cloud partnerships** (e.g., AWS or Google Cloud sustainability programs) and **carbon offset initiatives** like reforestation or verified credit schemes.\n\n- **Governance & Standards:** Oversight by a **sustainability committee**, environmental audits, and compliance with standards such as **ISO 14001** or **PAS 2060**.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates a measurable, transparent effort\nto **reduce AI's environmental impact** through efficiency, renewable\nenergy, and responsible lifecycle management.",
  "SU-2": "To comply with **SU-2 (\"Have you explored opportunities to optimize\nalgorithms for greater computational efficiency?\")**, organizations must\nshow they **actively reduce the computational and energy demands** of\ntheir AI systems through algorithmic and infrastructure optimization.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A formal **Efficiency Optimization Policy** defining goals for minimizing computational load and energy use across AI lifecycles.\n\n- **Algorithm Design & Development:** Use of **lightweight models** (e.g., MobileNet, DistilBERT) and **techniques such as pruning, quantization, and knowledge distillation** to cut unnecessary parameters while maintaining accuracy.\n\n- **Tools & Frameworks:** Implementation of **optimization toolkits** like *TensorFlow Model Optimization Toolkit*, *PyTorch Profiler*, or *ONNX* for efficient model deployment.\n\n- **Metrics & Validation:** Reports comparing **resource consumption before and after optimization**, with proof that accuracy and reliability remain stable.\n\n- **Monitoring & Continuous Improvement:** Ongoing tracking of **GPU/CPU utilization, energy use, and latency**, leading to iterative refinements.\n\n- **Infrastructure Efficiency:** Adoption of **energy-efficient hardware (TPUs, optimized GPUs)** and **cloud auto-scaling features** to prevent idle resource waste.\n\n- **Governance & Standards:** Oversight through efficiency review committees and adherence to **ISO 14001** and energy-efficient AI design principles.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates a deliberate, measurable effort to\n**optimize algorithms for sustainability, cost-effectiveness, and\nperformance efficiency**.",
  "SU-3": "To comply with **SU-3 (\"Are you using cloud services or infrastructure\nthat prioritizes renewable energy sources?\")**, organizations must show\nthat **cloud and infrastructure choices intentionally align with\nsustainability goals** by prioritizing renewable energy--powered\nproviders.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A formal **Sustainability Policy** committing to renewable energy use in infrastructure and cloud decisions.\n\n- **Cloud Provider Selection:** Evidence that selected providers (e.g., *Google Cloud*, *AWS*, *Microsoft Azure*) have **carbon-neutral or renewable energy certifications** such as *RECs*, *CarbonNeutral®*, or *Green-e*.\n\n- **Energy Source Documentation:** Records verifying renewable power usage in data centres or vendor contracts explicitly mandating green energy sourcing.\n\n- **Metrics & Monitoring:** Regular **energy usage and sustainability reports** tracking renewable energy proportions and comparing performance to industry benchmarks.\n\n- **Optimization Practices:** Proof of **energy-efficient deployment strategies**like auto-scaling, region selection based on renewable availability, and the use of cloud tools (e.g., *Google Cloud's Carbon Footprint* or *AWS Customer Carbon Tool*).\n\n- **Governance & Compliance:** Oversight via **sustainability committees** and alignment with **ISO 14001** or **ISO 50001** energy management standards.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates a proactive, measurable approach\nto **reducing carbon impact** by selecting and operating within cloud\nenvironments powered by renewable energy.",
  "SU-4": "To comply with **SU-4 (\"What is the estimated carbon footprint of\ntraining and deploying your AI system?\")**, organizations must\n**quantify, monitor, and mitigate greenhouse gas emissions** associated\nwith AI training, inference, and operation.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A formal **Sustainability or Carbon Management Policy** with measurable goals for reducing AI-related emissions.\n\n- **Carbon Assessment Reports:** Detailed **calculations of energy consumption and CO₂ equivalent emissions**, using tools such as *CodeCarbon*, *Green Algorithms*, or *Google Cloud's Carbon Footprint*.\n\n- **Metrics & Monitoring:** Tracking **energy usage (kWh)**, grid emission factors, and benchmarking results against internal or industry standards.\n\n- **Optimization Strategies:** Evidence of **energy-efficient model design** (e.g., pruning, quantization, or lightweight architectures) and **green infrastructure use** (e.g., renewable-powered data centres).\n\n- **Offsets & Mitigation:** Participation in **carbon offset programs**, renewable energy investments, or reforestation projects to compensate for emissions.\n\n- **Governance & Compliance:** Oversight by **sustainability committees**, audits validating emission data, and alignment with standards such as **ISO 14064** and the **GHG Protocol**.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates an evidence-based effort to\n**measure, reduce, and offset AI-related carbon emissions**, ensuring\nresponsible environmental stewardship throughout the AI lifecycle.",
  "SU-5": "To comply with **SU-5 (\"Have you optimized your algorithms to reduce\ncomputational resource requirements?\")**, organizations must show that\nthey've **systematically improved algorithmic efficiency** to minimize\nhardware, energy, and cost demands while preserving performance.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A formal **Efficiency Optimization Policy** defining measurable goals for reducing compute and energy usage.\n\n- **Algorithmic Optimization:** Proof of applying techniques such as **model pruning**, **quantization**, **knowledge distillation**, and use of **lightweight architectures** (e.g., *MobileNet*, *DistilBERT*).\n\n- **Tools & Frameworks:** Use of optimization toolkits like **TensorFlow Model Optimization Toolkit**, **PyTorch Quantization Toolkit**, or **ONNX Runtime**, plus profiling tools (e.g., *TensorBoard*, *NVIDIA Nsight*).\n\n- **Metrics & Validation:** Reports showing **reductions in memory, GPU/CPU load, and inference latency**, with trade-off analyses confirming accuracy retention.\n\n- **Deployment & Monitoring:** Evidence of **resource-efficient deployments** (e.g., serverless, containerized, batched inference) and continuous monitoring of compute performance.\n\n- **Governance & Compliance:** Oversight through internal review committees and alignment with **energy-efficient AI best practices** and **ISO 14001** sustainability principles.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates a structured, data-driven approach\nto **algorithm optimization**, achieving measurable efficiency gains\nthat reduce environmental impact and operational costs without degrading\nmodel quality.",
  "SU-6": "To comply with **SU-6 (\"Are you using hardware or cloud platforms that\nprioritize energy efficiency?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that\ntheir **AI infrastructure is intentionally chosen and managed for\nminimal energy consumption and environmental impact**.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A formal **Energy Efficiency Policy** linking hardware and infrastructure decisions to sustainability objectives.\n\n- **Energy-Efficient Hardware:** Proof of using **energy-optimized GPUs, CPUs, or TPUs**, ideally with **ENERGY STAR** or equivalent certifications, and documentation of workload optimization or cooling efficiency.\n\n- **Cloud Platform Selection:** Evidence that cloud providers hold **ISO 50001**, **CarbonNeutral®**, or similar certifications, and publish sustainability reports confirming energy-efficient data-center operations.\n\n- **Metrics & Monitoring:** Regularly tracked **energy-usage data and efficiency benchmarks** for hardware and cloud resources.\n\n- **Optimization & Renewable Integration:** Documentation of **load balancing, auto-scaling**, or **virtualization strategies**, and confirmation that data centres use or offset **renewable energy**.\n\n- **Governance & Compliance:** Oversight via sustainability committees, periodic energy audits, and adherence to **ISO 50001** or **ASHRAE** efficiency standards.\n\n**In short:** Compliance shows a measurable, policy-driven commitment to\n**reducing the energy footprint of AI operations** through efficient\nhardware choices, renewable-powered cloud infrastructure, and continuous\nperformance monitoring.",
  "SU-7": "To comply with **SU-7 (\"How do you balance sustainability goals with\nperformance requirements?\")**, organizations must demonstrate a\n**structured, evidence-based approach** to managing trade-offs between\n**energy efficiency** and **system performance** throughout the AI\nlifecycle.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A formal **Sustainability and Performance Policy** setting clear goals for both efficiency and output quality.\n\n- **Trade-Off Frameworks:** Documentation of **structured decision frameworks or matrices** evaluating sustainability versus performance impacts during design and deployment.\n\n- **Algorithm Optimization:** Proof that techniques like **model pruning, quantization, or knowledge distillation** are used to lower computational demand without degrading accuracy.\n\n- **Metrics & Monitoring:** Dual tracking of **energy consumption, carbon footprint, inference speed, and accuracy**, with real-time dashboards and historical comparisons.\n\n- **Infrastructure Efficiency:** Use of **energy-optimized hardware (e.g., TPUs)** and **cloud features** such as auto-scaling or energy-aware regions to maintain balance.\n\n- **Governance & Review:** Oversight by **cross-functional committees** ensuring sustainability is embedded in performance decisions, supported by audits and periodic reviews.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the organization deliberately\nmanages **sustainability-performance trade-offs**, ensuring high system\neffectiveness with minimized environmental impact through measurable,\ntransparent, and continuously improved practices.",
  "SU-8": "To comply with **SU-8 (\"Do you track the environmental impact of your AI\nsystems over time?\")**, organizations must show a **sustained,\ndata-driven approach** to monitoring and improving the environmental\nperformance of their AI operations.\n\n**Key evidence types:**\n\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A documented **Environmental Tracking Policy** outlining goals for long-term impact monitoring and footprint reduction.\n\n- **Metrics & Monitoring:** Continuous tracking of **energy usage, carbon emissions, and data center efficiency (e.g., PUE)** using tools like *Google Cloud Carbon Footprint*, *AWS Sustainability Dashboard*, or *CodeCarbon*.\n\n- **Data Collection & Analysis:** Historical datasets showing **trends and progress** in emissions reduction, including project-level energy tracking.\n\n- **Reporting & Transparency:** Regular **environmental reports** aligned with standards such as the **GHG Protocol** or **ISO 14064**, and stakeholder disclosures showing accountability.\n\n- **Optimization & Improvement:** Documentation showing that tracked data informs **algorithm, hardware, or workflow optimizations** to reduce impact.\n\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Evidence of **sustainability committees**, **audits**, and adherence to **ISO 50001 / ISO 14001** standards.\n\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the organization **actively\nmeasures, reports, and improves** the environmental footprint of its AI\nsystems, ensuring continuous alignment with sustainability goals and\nrecognized environmental standards."
};
